// The Cinder Sovereign (the Ultimate): the journal, every hero in their own voice (docs/plans/journal-voices.md). Acts I and II, the wall and Rebirth, and Act III (Corvane, Caelum Vey and Brannoc, then Grace Withdrawn with Nightfall, and Morrow-Oak's Seeds of Ash) and Act IV: Royal Doom (the cast that passes the doom on, later in the fight), Twin Brands read by charge, and Emberfall.
// The fight is told without words, so the pages never say what the Sovereign is; the heroes only say what they saw and which of the fallen they knew.
// Move keys are cast names (the journal fires on the cast bar's name). The echoes' moves are keyed by the move's own name: Flambé Service and Burning Web
// belong to the first two calls, Ember and Ash to Volkar's, The Crier (the bell, Royal Doom, the lantern and the knights' replay, one page) to the Hollow King's, then the Royal Tithe and the Dirge that ends the act.
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).sovereign = {
  // the first time this hero meets it (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "A tall figure on a column of smoke, a thin blade held low and an ember cupped in the other hand, and a ring of twelve flames turning over its head. It called the fallen up by name, and they came, burnt. I knew every one. Gave ground, but I know the ground now.",
    gloomsworn: "A tall, still figure with a dark face and two ember eyes, gliding on smoke. It spoke names, and the ones we buried came up out of the floor and knelt. I have seen the dead obey before. I had not seen them look so tired of it.",
    thresher: "Such a quiet, tall gentleman, dear, gliding on smoke with a little halo of flames. He calls the old guests by name and they come back scorched, and kneel, and do their dance. I know every name he said. Several are on my list already. Shall we begin?",
    bloodreaver: "The tall one just glides, thin blade low, ember in his other hand, and calls our old friends up out of the fire! I knew every one of them, my friend, and I'd have hit them harder if I'd known they'd be back. A good scar waiting for me.",
    runeguard: "A ring of twelve flames at its back, and it lights and lets one fly before each call. The answer, so far, is that every name it calls is one we have already read in the margin. Burnt now, and changed, and no less true to their form.",
    gorgemaw: "A tall, still host, gliding on smoke, with a blade held low and an ember cupped like a sweetmeat. It sends down to the cellar for my old acquaintances, and they come up scorched. Grubnak, flambéed! I have never wanted to taste a man's revenge so much. Still hungry.",
    oathbroker: "The counterparty is tall and still, and calls its creditors by name: each of those we settled with before comes back, scorched, to be paid again. It reads out the old clauses and they answer. I do not recall agreeing to renew. Entered in the ledger.",
    ironpalm: "It does not shout. It says a name, and the fallen answer. Each one I had listened to before and thought I understood. They spoke again, burnt, in a new voice, and I answered too slowly.",
    ranger: "Tall quarry on a column of smoke, thin blade low. Calls old kills up out of the floor by name, and they come back scorched. Knew every one. Spent the night counting the flames on the ring. Twelve. No shot yet.",
    shadowblade: "The mark is tall and still and has two ember eyes, and it never looks away, which is rude. It calls the ones we killed back up out of the floor, scorched and kneeling. I found them all familiar, and almost fond. Almost.",
    pyromancer: "He is on FIRE, and I mean to say so with respect! A tall, still thing with a thin blade, an ember in his hand and a ring of twelve flames overhead, calling our old kills back up blackened. Not one outshines me. Not one. I shall be gracious about it.",
    thermist: "Cold read: tall, still, a thin blade held low. Hot read: he calls out the names of everything we killed, and they come back burning, and I felt every one in my teeth. Ran hot all night. Cooler tomorrow.",
    driftblade: "A tall, still figure gliding on smoke, which I resent, since I never glide, and a ring of twelve flames turning overhead, and every time a flame flew down, one of the old ones came up through the floor and knelt and did something new, so I kept moving, through all of it.",
    alembist: "Specimen: tall, still, a dark face, two ember eyes. Observed: it calls out the fallen by name and they come back scorched. Note: I knew every one. Label: the ring of twelve flames overhead, and how many are dark by the end. Two lanterns lost, both accidental.",
    threadweaver: "A tall figure on a column of smoke, a blade as thin as a needle, and every name it calls pulls an old thread up out of the floor. I knew each one by the stitches. Burnt through now, and sewn into something new. Mind the slack.",
    marionettist: "Our star is tall, still, and gliding on smoke, with a ring of twelve flames for a halo, and calls the old company up by name: scorched, kneeling, word perfect and wearing new costumes. (Every one of them a familiar face. The Doll was unsettled.)",
    cleric: "Poor soul, so tall and still, with a dark face and two ember eyes. It calls out the fallen by name, and they come up scorched and kneel to it. I said a prayer for every one I knew, which was all of them. I hope the light finds them a gentler rest.",
    grovekeeper: "Tall and still, like the dead of winter with a fire in it. The old ones came up out of the floor when it spoke their names, scorched, like a field after burning. I knew them all. Something is different in them. A new growth.",
    chronomancer: "It calls the fallen by name, one flame at a time, and they come up burnt and answer in a new way. I thought I had heard every move of theirs before. I had not, which is the odd part. I felt rather cheated, and rather curious.",
    tidecaller: "A tall, still shore of a figure, and the fallen come in on it like surf, scorched, each called by name. I knew every wave. They broke in a new way, this time. I stood in the hall a long while, learning the ring of twelve flames.",
    lanternkeeper: "A tall figure on smoke, with twelve flames turning in a ring over its head, and every flame that flew down became one of the old ones, scorched. I counted the flames, and the dark ones, and went home thinking about the corners I had not lit.",
    kenseier: "A tall, still swordsman, one thin blade held low. It calls the fallen by name and each one answers, scorched, in a new form. I knew every one of them, and none of their old counts held. Tomorrow I learn the new ones.",
    houndmaster: "Tall lad on a column of smoke, thin blade low. Calls our old kills up by name and they come back singed. Ash stayed behind my knees the whole time. Know every one of them, mind, bar the new tricks. Stay.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats it
  win: {
    bastion: "It is down. The ring of flames went dark one by one, and the fallen did not rise again. The last of them lay down by his lantern. We held the ground to the end.",
    gloomsworn: "It is down. The flames on the ring went out, and the old ones stayed down, and the last of them lay beside his lantern. I know a watch relieved when I see one.",
    thresher: "It is down, dear, and the flames are all out, and every name it called is quiet again. The last guest lay down beside his lantern, as sweet as you please. I hummed him the rest of the way.",
    bloodreaver: "It's down! The flames went out one by one, and the old ones lay down and stayed down, and the King set his lantern on the stones and lay beside it. I bled a good deal for that, my friend.",
    runeguard: "It is fallen, and entered in the margin: the twelve flames dark, the fallen at rest, and the last of them beside his lantern. Every call answered, every dance foreseen in the end.",
    gorgemaw: "The last course is cleared, the ring of flames dark, and all my old acquaintances returned to their graves. The King put down his lantern and lay beside it. A long tasting menu, and I finished every course.",
    oathbroker: "The Sovereign is concluded: the twelve flames dark, the fallen released, and the last of them laid down beside his lantern. All accounts discharged.",
    ironpalm: "It fell, and the flames went out, one after another, and every voice it had called fell quiet. The last of them set down his lantern and lay beside it. We had answered every word.",
    ranger: "Down. Twelve flames dark. The old ones stayed down. Last of them put down his lantern and lay beside it. A hard trail, ended.",
    shadowblade: "It is down, and the flames are out, and the ones it called lay down again, which was kind of them. The last put down his lantern and lay beside it. It never looked my way, and in the end there was no one left to look.",
    pyromancer: "He's down, and every flame on his ring went out, and the old ones lay down and stayed down! Even the lantern was set on the stones. I counted the ring going dark flame by flame and clapped, which nobody else did.",
    thermist: "Down. Flames out, all twelve. The old ones lay down, the last by his lantern. The hall cooled for the first time all night, and I stood in it a long while, glad.",
    driftblade: "It's down, and all the flames on its ring went out, and the old ones lay down where they stood, and the last of them put his lantern down on the stones and lay beside it, and I went round the whole room once more, slowly, just to see it still.",
    alembist: "Specimen: down. Observed: the twelve flames dark, the fallen at rest, the last of them beside his lantern. Note: nothing left on the shelf for the fire. Label: finished. The explosion was, for once, entirely intended.",
    threadweaver: "It is down, and every thread it pulled up came loose and lay slack. The last of them set down his lantern and lay beside it. A hard seam, and tied off at last.",
    marionettist: "Our star is down, the ring of twelve flames dark, and the old company laid down, and last of all the old King, who put down his lantern and lay beside it. (Curtain. The Doll bowed. I did not.)",
    cleric: "It is down, the poor soul, and the flames on the ring went out. The old ones lay down and did not rise, and the last of them set his lantern on the stones and lay beside it. I knelt by him a while.",
    grovekeeper: "It is down, and the burning is over. The flames went out one by one, and the old ones lay down in the ash, the last beside his lantern. The ground will grow green again. Sit. Tea.",
    chronomancer: "It fell, and nothing needed undoing, which is a first. The twelve flames went out and the fallen lay down, the last beside his lantern. I watched it end, and for once was not early for anything.",
    tidecaller: "It is down, and the flames drew off one by one, the way a shore dries. The old ones lay down on the shingle, the last beside his lantern. I sang low, over all of them. Even a fire is only weather, and it passes.",
    lanternkeeper: "It is down, and I watched the twelve flames go out, one after another. The last of the old ones set his lantern down on the stones and lay beside it. I left it burning.",
    kenseier: "It is down, the flames dark, the fallen at rest, and the last of them set down his lantern and lay beside it. Every beat of that dance was honest, once I could count it.",
    houndmaster: "Tall lad's down, flames out, the old ones laid down and stayed down. Last one set his lantern on the stones and lay by it. Both dogs came through without a mark. Stood a while and watched the ash fall.",
  },
  moves: {
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------- the Sovereign's own moves
    'Kindling': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The whole hall lit at once, and there was nothing to block. I planted my feet, let the line close up beside the healer, and we took it together.",
      gloomsworn: "Fire went through every one of us at once, and no one could stand out of it. I took it whole and stayed where I was. Fire is only warmth to the dead. Our healer had the real work of it.",
      thresher: "The whole hall went up at once, dear, and every one of us was on the list. So we gathered close to our healers and let it pass together. Do mind your coat-hem.",
      bloodreaver: "Fire across the whole floor and no wall to hide behind! I took my share and liked it, my friend, and kept near the healer so the rest could be mended in one go. Bleed first, drink after.",
      runeguard: "The halo flared bright and the hem of the robe lifted; that is the sign. Close ranks beside the healer before it breaks, since a blast that reaches all of us can only be shared.",
      gorgemaw: "A hot course served to the entire table at once, with no plate to hide behind. I opened wide beside the healer and swallowed my share. A touch smoky for my palate.",
      oathbroker: "The clause is plain: owed by all the undersigned together, no exemption and no appeal. We stood close to the healer and paid it in common, and the healer settled the balance after.",
      ironpalm: "It spoke to everyone at once, loudly, and no hand could turn it. We gathered close and listened together. The healer answered after.",
      ranger: "Whole hall, no cover, no lane. Closed on the healer, took it, drew again as the smoke cleared. No line to be had, so no loss in taking it.",
      shadowblade: "The mark lit the whole hall, which was thoughtless of it: there was no dark corner left to be unseen in. I stood by the healer like everyone else and took it. It saw everyone, which was rather the point.",
      pyromancer: "Fire everywhere, and not even mine! I stood by the healer, cast straight through it and let the glow wash over the whole stage. It is rude to be outshone in your own colour.",
      thermist: "Heat. All of it, everywhere, no wall. Flushed, then settled. In close to the healer, and let it wash over all of us together. It ends. It always ends.",
      driftblade: "It filled the whole hall and there was no through to go through, so I swallowed the urge to run and stood close among the rest, the healer's light warm at my back, until it was past, and then I was off round him again.",
      alembist: "Symptom: the whole hall scorched at once, every time the Sovereign opens a new act. Cure: everyone gathered close, the brew steeped in the quiet before it. A kitchen fire, but not mine this time.",
      threadweaver: "The whole room at once, so no one could be tied to anyone else's hem. I drew my stitches in close and pulled them tight, and we took it together.",
      marionettist: "Our star lit the whole house at once. Nowhere to hide, so the company gathered downstage by the healer and took it as one. (Curtain, scorched. Applause, thin.)",
      cleric: "The whole hall burned at once, and nobody could stand out of it. I called everyone in close first, so one prayer could reach them all. Quiet thanks, after.",
      grovekeeper: "It came on like a grass fire across the whole floor. I gathered my lot in close and sowed the mending before it broke, and reaped after.",
      chronomancer: "The hall burned all at once, on the very first beat, as it always does. I gathered everyone a breath beforehand and kept time for them. I'll have known it was coming.",
      tidecaller: "A swell across the whole hall, and no shore to stand behind. I brought the water up before it came, so it was high when it broke and all of us stood in it together.",
      lanternkeeper: "The whole hall lit at once, and I had hung my light on each of them beforehand. Four of us, close together, one glow to share. It burned through all of us and went out.",
      kenseier: "A blast across the whole hall, and a blade cannot turn it. We drew in beside the healer and stood in it together. It keeps good time and opens every act the same way.",
      houndmaster: "Fire across the whole floor, dogs and all. No running from it. Whistled the pair in tight by the healer and sat it out together.",
    } },
    'Brand': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The blade came for me alone, and the cut left a burn that stayed. I braced as its shoulder dropped. The next cut inside the burn lands harder, so I kept my best guard for that one.",
      gloomsworn: "The blade was meant for me, and the wound it leaves keeps burning. The next cut inside the burn is worse, so I drew the dark in early for that one. A burn is only a burn. The healer minded it more than I did.",
      thresher: "He cuts our knight, dear, and the cut smoulders a good long while. The next cut that finds the smoulder is the heavy one, so we mind the gap. I stayed clear of the ring at the knight's feet and hummed.",
      bloodreaver: "The blade came for me, and the burn stayed on, still biting! The next cut inside it was worse, so I saved my best guard for that and drank the rest in. A fine scar, and still warm.",
      runeguard: "The blade lifts and a ring is drawn at my feet; so it begins. Take the cut, mark that the burn will keep, and save the great ward for the next cut inside it, which lands heavier.",
      gorgemaw: "One slice, plated for me alone, and it goes on cooking. The next slice inside the burn is the rich one, so I kept my best mouthful for it. Nicely seared.",
      oathbroker: "The cut is payable by whoever stands in front, and the burn it leaves is an open account. The fine print: the next cut inside the burn is charged heavier, so I deferred my largest guard to meet it.",
      ironpalm: "The blade spoke to me alone. I took the word, and the burn stayed on like an echo. The next word inside the echo is louder, so I held my breath for that one.",
      ranger: "Blade went for our shield-man, ring drawn at his feet. Kept out of it. The burn lingers, and the next cut inside it lands harder. Kept clear both times.",
      shadowblade: "It cut our shield and left a burn behind, which is messy. The next cut inside the burn is heavier, which is worse manners still. I kept to its flank, out of the ring, and said nothing.",
      pyromancer: "He branded our knight in a ring of light, and it kept burning! I stayed out of the ring and went on casting. The next cut inside the burn hits harder, so the knight took care, and I admired his restraint.",
      thermist: "One cut, one ring, whoever stands in front. Then the burn stayed and spread like a fever, and the next cut inside it is the heavy one. I stayed out of the ring, close behind.",
      driftblade: "The blade came down on our shield-man in a ring drawn on the floor, and the burn stayed on him, and the next cut inside it was heavier, so I went round the ring and through the gap behind and never once stood in it.",
      alembist: "A cut for the knight alone, and a burn that stays. The next cut inside the burn lands heavy, so I steeped a guard for it and kept mending through the long smoulder. Note for the shelf: I stood outside the ring.",
      threadweaver: "The blade cut our knight and left a burn running in the seam, and the next cut inside the burn is the one that tears. I kept my needle ready for that and mended while it ran.",
      marionettist: "Our star cut at the leading man's mark, and left a burn on him like a smear of greasepaint. The next cut inside it is the big scene, so he saved his best defence for it. (The rest of the company stayed out of the ring.)",
      cleric: "The blade fell on our knight, as it always does, and the burn it left stayed on him. I laid the light on him before the next cut instead of after, since that one is heavier.",
      grovekeeper: "The blade came for our knight and left a burn that kept on, like a root fire. I tended him through it, and planted before the next cut, since a second one inside the burn lands harder.",
      chronomancer: "A cut for our knight, and the burn it left lasted; the next cut inside it is the one that matters. I mended him a moment ahead, so he was already whole when it came. He never noticed. Nobody does.",
      tidecaller: "The blade came for our knight, and the burn it left ran on like a slow current. I raised the water on him again before the next cut, since a cut inside the burn lands on a heavier swell.",
      lanternkeeper: "The blade cut our knight and left a glow on him that would not go out. I hung a lantern on him and left it burning, since the next cut inside the glow is heavier. A small steady light beats a clever one that comes late.",
      kenseier: "The blade came for me, on a beat I could count. I met it as it landed, and the burn stayed on. The next cut inside the burn is heavier, so I drew the ash about me for that one. Honest, for a sovereign.",
      houndmaster: "Cut went for the shield-man, ring on the floor. Dogs stayed out of it. The burn stays, and the next cut inside it lands harder, so he'd saved his best guard for it. Down, the pair of you. Stay.",
    } },
    'Ashen Sweep': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The blade covered half the hall, on whichever side it was held, and before some sweeps it passed to the other hand. I watched the hand, then took the empty side, and the line followed.",
      gloomsworn: "Half the room, on the side where the blade was held. Sometimes it changed hands first, and that was the warning. I stood on the empty side, as the living ought.",
      thresher: "Half the room goes, dear, whichever side the blade is held, and sometimes the blade passes to the other hand beforehand, like a hint. Watch the hand, step to the empty side.",
      bloodreaver: "The big one swept half the hall, on the blade's side, and I let him know I noticed it changed hands first! Watch the hand, take the empty half. That wound I did not need. A shameful miss, for him.",
      runeguard: "A sweep is preceded by the blade passing to the other hand, or staying. The half of the room on the side of the blade is struck; the empty side is safe. Read the hand, then go to the empty half.",
      gorgemaw: "A great half-moon carved out of the room, on the side of the blade. Sometimes my host swaps hands before carving, which is theatrical. I took the side of the empty hand. A lot of flourish for so little flavour.",
      oathbroker: "Clause: half the room, on the side where the blade is held. Of note, the blade changes hands first, at times, and the change is the notice. We moved to the empty side before judgment was given.",
      ironpalm: "The blade spoke to half the room, on its own side. First it changed hands, a pause, then the word. We stood where the blade was not.",
      ranger: "Half the room, blade's side. Sometimes it swaps hands first. Watch the hand, go to the empty side. Never take the side with the blade.",
      shadowblade: "It swept half the room on the blade's side, and swapped hands before some of them, which is a lovely warning. I went to the other side without a sound.",
      pyromancer: "Half the hall, in a glowing slab on the blade's side, and he changed hands first like a showman! I watched the hand, stepped to the empty half, and went on casting.",
      thermist: "The blade is in one hand. Half the room is hot, the other half is not. Step to the cold half, and when it changes hands, change sides, quickly, before the heat catches the wrong foot.",
      driftblade: "The blade changed hands and I saw it and went through the middle of the room to the other half, through the line where it would have been and out the far side, and the sweep took half the hall behind me and never touched me.",
      alembist: "Half the room scorched, on the side of the blade. Watch which hand holds it, and go to the empty side. It sometimes changes hands first, which is the tell, and a good one.",
      threadweaver: "Half the room cut away on the blade's side, like a bad hem. It changes hands before some of them. I watched the hand and pulled my stitches to the whole side.",
      marionettist: "Our star carved half the stage, on the side of the blade. Sometimes he passes it to the other hand first: a cue. The company moved to the empty side on it. (A tidy exit, stage left.)",
      cleric: "Half the hall swept away on the blade's side. When it changes hands, that is the sign. I called everyone to the empty side before the light left his hand, and none of us was hurt.",
      grovekeeper: "It swept half the hall like a scythe across a field, on the side of the blade. When it changed hands, I knew which half would be left standing. I gathered my lot there.",
      chronomancer: "The blade changes hands just before it sweeps, which is the sort of order I admire. Half the room goes, the half the blade is on. We were on the other half, as we are every time.",
      tidecaller: "A wave over half the hall, on the side of the blade, and the blade passes from hand to hand before it comes, the way the water draws back before a big breaker. I stood on the dry half, and the rest followed.",
      lanternkeeper: "The sweep took half the hall, on the side of the blade, and the blade changes hands before some. I lit the empty side and stood there, and the rest came to the light. One on the floor, three of us.",
      kenseier: "A sweep across half the hall, on the blade's side. The hand it passes to is the first beat, and the sweep the second. A blade cannot turn it, so I stepped through to the empty side. He keeps good time.",
      houndmaster: "Half the hall, on the blade's side, and before some he passes it to the other hand. Watched the hand, walked the lads round to the empty side.",
    } },
    'Cupped Ember': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The ember in its hand was thrown at one of the others, and a blue ring settled on them. It wanted sharing. I stepped in with the rest and we took it together, so no one had it alone.",
      gloomsworn: "It threw the ember it had been cupping, at one of us, and a blue ring settled where they stood. It is not a blow for one to take. I walked in with the rest. A shared wound is a lighter one.",
      thresher: "He threw his little cupped ember at one of us, dear, and drew a blue ring round where it would land. The kind thing is to share it, so everyone steps into the ring. In we go, all together.",
      bloodreaver: "It threw the ember at somebody who wasn't me, which I resent! Blue ring on the floor. A hit like that wants sharing, so I stepped in, and everyone else did too, and we took it as a family.",
      runeguard: "He cups the ember and turns his hand, and the sign is unmistakable. It is thrown at one of the others, in a blue ring, and the blow is made to be shared. Every one of us stepped into the ring, and it was divided.",
      gorgemaw: "A single ember, tossed at one of my friends like a dumpling, a blue ring drawn round the landing. A dish meant for sharing. We all stepped into the ring and ate it together. Hot, but filling.",
      oathbroker: "The terms: the ember lands on one of us, and the ring drawn in blue means the debt is to be shared in common. Anyone outside the ring pays nothing, and anyone alone pays it all. We stood together.",
      ironpalm: "He threw a word at one of us, and it was meant to be heard together. We stepped into the blue ring and took it as one voice.",
      ranger: "Ember thrown at one of us, blue ring where it lands. Meant to be shared. Closed in on them, all of us, and took it together.",
      shadowblade: "It threw its ember at someone, which is hardly subtle, and a blue ring settled where it would land. I stepped in with the rest. It was the one time all of us were seen at once.",
      pyromancer: "He threw the ember at someone else, and a blue ring appeared to show where! Fire meant for sharing, so I stood in the ring with everyone else and cast on from there. Shared glory, and I bore it nobly.",
      thermist: "A blue ring, one target, a hit meant to be divided. Step in, all of us. Shared, the heat is only warmth, and it passed.",
      driftblade: "The ember flew at someone and a blue ring went down round them and the whole thing wanted sharing, so I ran in through the ring and out the far side and in again, until the last breath, and then I stood in it with the rest.",
      alembist: "One ember, thrown at one of us, meant to be borne by all. Everyone close in the blue ring, so the dose divides. Not a good moment to be alone. Shared, not spilt.",
      threadweaver: "He threw the ember at one of us, and I tied my stitches to that one, all of us pulled in together, and it spread the weight between four. A neat seam, if I say so myself.",
      marionettist: "Our star threw the ember to one of the company, and a blue ring drew the spotlight. We joined them on the mark, all together. (A group number. Brief, and warm.)",
      cleric: "The ember was thrown at one of our friends, and a blue ring settled round them. I called everyone in at once, so the weight of it was shared. It was gentler that way.",
      grovekeeper: "He threw the ember like a seed at one of my lot, and the blue ring marked the soil. I gathered everyone into it, since a hit shared is a lighter one. Planted together, grew together.",
      chronomancer: "The ember went to one of us, and a blue ring told us where, ahead of the throw. We all went in and shared it. My share was taken before I arrived. It did not take long.",
      tidecaller: "The ember flew like a spray, to one of us, and a blue ring marked the place. We all went in together, since a wave divided is a small one. The water was ready before it landed.",
      lanternkeeper: "The ember was thrown at one of us, and a blue ring settled round them. I picked up my light and set it down inside the ring, and the rest walked in after. Four of us, one ring, enough to go round.",
      kenseier: "The ember came on a beat I could count, at one of the others, in a blue ring. A blade cannot turn a thing meant to be shared. We stood in it together. A courtesy, for a sovereign.",
      houndmaster: "Ember thrown at one of ours, blue ring on the floor. Meant to be shared, so the dogs and I went in with the rest and sat it out together.",
    } },
    'Scatter': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A red mark settled over each of us, and each needed our own patch of floor. No one stood within reach of another, and the line broke into pieces. Held, apart.",
      gloomsworn: "Every one of us marked, and each marked place needing room. I found my own bare ground and stayed on it. A man who has been buried does not mind a small space, but he minds a crowded one.",
      thresher: "A little red circle over each of us, dear, and each wanted its own piece of floor. So off we went to our separate places, nobody's circle touching another's.",
      bloodreaver: "Marks over every head, mine too! Each one burns on its own, so none of us could stand near another. I took my own patch of floor and a good scar, if I'm honest. Nothing shared.",
      runeguard: "A ring appears over each of us, and none may touch another; the inscription is plain. Each went to a place apart, mine the last to settle. The marks were drawn plainly; so was the room.",
      gorgemaw: "One small helping for each of us, red, hot and personal. None to be shared. We each took a separate plate of floor and ate our own. Mild, and it went on a little long.",
      oathbroker: "Item: a separate debt on each of us, and no two to be paid together. We took separate ground and settled individually. Rings which touch are charged double.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke to each of us alone, and each had to listen alone. We spread out, one by one, and let each word land on its own.",
      ranger: "Mark over each of us, each wanting its own ground. Fanned out like a hunting party, nobody's ring touching another's. Shot through the gaps.",
      shadowblade: "A red mark on each of us, and none could be near another, which suited me, since I prefer to be alone. I took my own corner of the floor and kept it.",
      pyromancer: "A mark over every single head, mine glowing brightest, obviously! None of them can touch, so we each took a corner and I cast on from mine. Personally marked, personally spared.",
      thermist: "One ring on each of us, no overlap. Step apart, each to our own patch. Too close and it burned twice. Too far and I was alone, shivering. Apart, but not too far.",
      driftblade: "A mark over each of us, and each needed room, which I have always wanted, so I ran out to my own patch and circled it and the rest did the same, and none of the rings touched, and I was gone through the gap before it went.",
      alembist: "Note for the shelf: the marks over our heads must not touch. Each took a separate patch of floor, and I threw the mending into the middle of us after. Only one small fire, deliberate.",
      threadweaver: "A ring over each of my stitches, and no two to touch, so I cut the threads before they snapped and let each go to a separate patch of floor. Tied them again after.",
      marionettist: "A spotlight on each of the company, and each needed a stage of their own. We went to separate places, nobody's light crossing another's. (A solo for each, and no duets.)",
      cleric: "A red mark over each of my dear ones, and each wanted a patch of floor of their own. I told them kindly to spread out, and they did, and I mended each where they stood.",
      grovekeeper: "A ring over every one of my lot, each needing room, like seedlings thinned. I sent them out to their own ground and sowed the mending after. Give them room first.",
      chronomancer: "Every one of us marked, each needing their own floor, and the marks come well before the burning. We stepped away on the count. I stood alone, all along, or will. It was fine.",
      tidecaller: "Every one of us marked, and each needing a patch of floor, like rock pools. We drew apart and I poured a little of the sea into each. Nobody needed to be quick.",
      lanternkeeper: "A mark over each of us, and each needed a patch of floor of their own. I walked ahead, lit four small lamps in four corners, and everyone went to their own. A patch each, and a lamp on every patch.",
      kenseier: "A mark over each of us, on a beat I could count, and each ring burning alone. A blade cannot turn four rings. We each took our own ground and kept it.",
      houndmaster: "Marks over every head, mine and the dogs' near enough. Each wanted its own bit of floor. Sent Rust one way and Ash the other, and walked off to my own.",
    } },
    'Halo Turn': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The ring of flames over its head turned, and from each flame still lit a line was drawn outward across the floor. The dark ones fired nothing. I stood between two lines and kept the line behind me there.",
      gloomsworn: "The flames on the halo turned, and a line ran out from each one that still burned. The dark ones were silent. I found the gap between two lines and stayed in it. I know the sensation of narrow ground.",
      thresher: "The little halo turns, dear, and every flame still lit draws a line out across the floor. The dark ones have gone quiet. We stood in the gaps between the lines and waited for the hum to end.",
      bloodreaver: "The ring of flames turned and drew red lines across the floor, one from every flame still burning! I picked a gap between two lines and stood in it, and let them go by on either side. Not a scratch.",
      runeguard: "The halo turns, and each lit flame draws its line outward. The lines are drawn plainly, and the gaps between them are where we stand; the dark flames fire nothing.",
      gorgemaw: "My host carves the room into spokes like a pie, one for each flame still alight. The dark flames are slices already eaten. We stood in the gaps between spokes and let the knife pass.",
      oathbroker: "A line outward from each lit flame, drawn plainly and fired together. Dark flames owe nothing. Note that the lines run the full width of the floor. We stood in the gaps between and paid nothing.",
      ironpalm: "Each lit flame said its word along the floor, and the dark ones said nothing. I listened for the gaps between the words and stood in one.",
      ranger: "Halo turns, line drawn from each lit flame. Dark ones fire nothing. Gaps between lines are the lanes. Stood in one.",
      shadowblade: "The halo turned and each flame still lit drew a line outward, which is thorough of it. I stood in the gap between two, where it did not look.",
      pyromancer: "The halo turned and a line of light ran out from every flame still burning, and not one of them as bright as me! I stood in the gap between two and cast on from there. The dark flames had nothing to say.",
      thermist: "Lines from every lit flame, and none from the dark ones. Step into the gap. The more flames went dark, the wider the gaps, and the easier it got to breathe.",
      driftblade: "The halo turned and the lines went out from every lit flame, and I slipped into the gap between two of them and out the other side and into the next gap, feet barely down, and the lines closed behind me.",
      alembist: "A line from each lit flame, across the floor, all at once. Stand in the gap between lines. The more flames are dark, the wider the gaps, so it gets easier, in this one way.",
      threadweaver: "The halo turned and drew its seams across the floor, one for every flame still alight, and the gaps between them were the only whole cloth. We stood in the gaps, and minded the seams.",
      marionettist: "The halo turned, and every flame still lit threw a beam across the stage. (The dark ones are an empty seat.) The company took the gaps between the beams, and held for the cue. A tidy blocking.",
      cleric: "The ring above its head turned, and a line ran out from every flame still burning. I led them into the gaps between, and the lines passed on either side. The dark flames I commended quietly.",
      grovekeeper: "The halo turned like a wheel in the wind, and each flame still lit drew a line like a furrow. We stood between the furrows. The dark ones were already fallow. Sow between the rows.",
      chronomancer: "The halo turns, the lines are drawn, and a moment later they fire. We stood between them while they waited. The more flames go dark, the wider the gaps, which will have been a comfort. It was.",
      tidecaller: "The halo turned slowly, like the moon pulling a tide, and a line ran out from every flame still lit. We stood in the slack water between. The dark flames pulled no more.",
      lanternkeeper: "The ring turned, and every flame still lit threw a line across the floor, like lamplight down a street. I found the dark gaps between and stood in them, and the rest came after. The gaps were wide enough for all of us.",
      kenseier: "The halo turned, and each lit flame drew a line, on a beat I could count. A blade cannot turn a dozen lines. We stood in the gaps between them and let them pass. The dark flames were a rest between strokes.",
      houndmaster: "Ring on top of him turned, and a line came out of every flame still burning. Walked the dogs into the gap between two. Sit. Lines passed either side.",
    } },
    'Halo Procession': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two arcs of the halo lit and swept round the floor, and I kept my feet ahead of them with the group beside me. When a ring closed over one of us, that one left the line and took the burst alone. Then back together, and the ember.",
      gloomsworn: "Two lit arcs of the halo went round the room like slow hands, and we walked before them in step. A ring over each of us closed in turn, and its bearer went apart to burn alone. I am used to going apart. Then we gathered at the ember.",
      thresher: "The little halo lit two arcs and chased us round the floor, dear, and we trotted ahead like ducklings. Each of us had a ring that closed in turn, and whoever's it was stepped away to burst, and came back. Then the ember, and cuddling up.",
      bloodreaver: "Two arcs of light came round after us and I made them chase! Every one of us had a ring that closed in turn, and when mine closed I went out alone and burst, and no friend was scorched. Back to the group, and a big ember to share.",
      runeguard: "Two arcs of the halo light and turn, and how they begin shows which way they go. Walk ahead of them as one, and when your ring closes, step out and burst alone, each in order. The ember ends it.",
      gorgemaw: "Two carving knives of light went round the floor, and we kept ahead of the blades. Each of us had a ring that closed in its own course, and when it closed, one stepped out of the party to boil over alone. A tidy service. The ember was the pudding.",
      oathbroker: "The clause: two arcs of light turn round the room, the direction shown at the start, and the party walks ahead of them. Each hero's ring closes in turn and bursts on that hero, who stands apart for it and returns. The ember is the settlement.",
      ironpalm: "Two arcs of the halo turned, and we walked ahead of them as one, breathing together. Each ring closed in its own time, and its bearer stepped apart, burst, and came home. Then the ember, held out for all. Nothing hurried.",
      ranger: "Two arcs lit, turning. Direction shows in the first inch. Walked ahead, group tight. Each ring closes in turn: bearer steps clear, bursts alone, comes back. Ember ends it.",
      shadowblade: "Two arcs of light came round the floor, and I stayed a step ahead of them, in whatever dark was left. When my ring closed I slipped out of the group and burst where nobody stood. Then back, and the ember. Nobody remarked on my return.",
      pyromancer: "Two arcs of the halo swept the floor after us and I walked ahead of them, crackling! When my ring closed I stepped out of the party and burst, and came through whole. Then the ember, a small one, and I did not mind sharing.",
      thermist: "Two arcs of light sweep round and we keep ahead of them. Each ring closes in turn, and its bearer stands off and bursts alone, then rejoins the group. The ember closes it all, hot and cold together.",
      driftblade: "Two arcs of light came round the floor and I drifted ahead of them, never stopping, and when my ring closed I drifted out of the group, burst, and drifted back in as though I had never left. Then the ember, and everyone's elbows.",
      alembist: "Two arcs of light sweep the floor, and a ring closes over each of us in turn. Walk ahead of the arcs, and when your ring closes, step clear of the group to burst, then return. The ember is the last dose; take them in order.",
      threadweaver: "Two seams of light were drawn round the floor, and we kept stitching ahead of them, all four together. When a ring closed over one of us, that one stepped out of the cloth to burst and was sewn back in after. The ember tied the knot.",
      marionettist: "Two spotlights swept the stage and the company walked ahead of them in a line. (A ring closed over each of us in turn, and that player took a solo, alone and unwatched, before rejoining the chorus.) The ember was the curtain call.",
      cleric: "Two arcs of light went round after us and I walked everyone ahead of it, one gentle step at a time. When a ring closed over one, that one went apart to burst, and I mended them when they came back. Then the ember, shared by all.",
      grovekeeper: "Two arcs of light went round the clearing like a scythe, and we walked ahead of the blade together. When a ring closed on one of my lot, that one stepped out to burst alone, then came back to the stand. Then the ember, and a mending after.",
      chronomancer: "Two arcs of light turned round the floor, and the way they began told me the way they would go, which is a rare kindness. Each of us had a ring that closed in turn: step out, burst, step back. I had done it already, some moments from now. The ember, and we gathered.",
      tidecaller: "Two arcs of light went round the floor, flooding in, and we walked ahead of the water. Each ring closed in its own time, and its bearer drew apart to burst, and came back with the ebb. Then the ember, and we gathered like pools, unhurried.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two arcs of the halo turned round the room, and I walked the party ahead of their light, lantern up. A ring closed over each of us in turn, and the bearer carried their own small fire aside and set it down, and came home. Then the ember, one lamp for all.",
      kenseier: "Two arcs of light turned round the room, and the first inch of their turn told me the whole of it. We walked ahead of the blades. Each ring closed on a beat I could count, and its bearer stepped out, burst alone, and returned. The ember closed the form.",
      houndmaster: "Two bright arcs came round the floor after us and we kept ahead, dogs and all. When a ring closed over me I took myself and the pups off to one side, burst, and trotted back. Then the ember, and everyone bundled in.",
    } },
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------- Act I's echoes
    'Flambé Service': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Four covered plates rose round the hall, one hero to a plate, served in the order their rims lit. I stood on mine, stepped off the line of fire as it left toward the next, and ended in the half where the last plate stood.",
      gloomsworn: "Grubnak, burnt, and four covered plates raised for the four of us, served in turn, fire running from each to the next. I stood on mine and stepped off its road after. I have been the dish before. Then to the last plate's half.",
      thresher: "The old cook, scorched, set four plates out, dear, one each, and lit them in order, a burning road from each to the next. Stand on yours till it is served, step off the road, and finish where the last plate stood.",
      bloodreaver: "The big cook came back burnt, and so pleased with himself! Four plates, one each, lit in turn, a line of fire from each to the next. I stood on mine, let it go off, stepped aside, and ran to the last plate's half.",
      runeguard: "Four covers rise, their rims filling in an order, and the order is the instruction. Each of us stood on one cover until it was served, then off the road of fire to the next, and at the end to the half where the last cover stood.",
      gorgemaw: "Grubnak himself on the flame! Four plates, one each, lit in turn, a burning line running to the next. I stood on mine, stepped off, and finished on the last plate's half of the hall. Overcooked, but honest. A dish I would have paid to see him eat.",
      oathbroker: "The old cook honours his old contract, burnt: four plates, one per signatory, served in the order the rims fill. Beneath it runs the line of fire from each to the next. We stepped off it and ended in the half where the last plate stood.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke slowly, burnt, in four plates, one of us to each, lit in order. I stood on mine until it was said, stepped off the road of fire, and went to the half where the last word fell.",
      ranger: "Old cook, burnt. Four plates, one each, lit in order, a line of fire from each to the next. Held mine till it lit, stepped off the line, then to the last plate's half.",
      shadowblade: "The old cook came up scorched, with four plates and a line of fire from each to the next. He did not see me stand on mine, nor leave it. I ended on the half where the last plate stood.",
      pyromancer: "FLAMBÉ! Grubnak, set alight, and a cook after my own heart, if he had any flair. Four plates, one each, lit in turn. I stood on mine, stepped off the line of fire, and finished in the last plate's half.",
      thermist: "Four plates, one each, the order in the rims. Stand on yours. A line of fire runs to the next, quick, and I was off it before I could think. Ended in the last plate's half.",
      driftblade: "Four plates, one each, and I stood on mine, which I hate, and the line of fire ran from it to the next and I went off it, sideways and through, and out to the half where the last plate stood, only just in time.",
      alembist: "Four covered plates, one hero to each, a line of fire from each to the next. Stand on yours, step off the line, finish in the half where the last plate stood. Grubnak, burnt, as I always said he would be.",
      threadweaver: "Four plates and a thread of fire sewn from each to the next, in the order the rims lit. I stood on mine, stepped off the seam, and ended on the side where the last plate stood. Neat work, for Grubnak.",
      marionettist: "The old chef, scorched, laid four plates round the stage, one to each of us, and a line of fire from each to the next. (Hold your mark until called, then exit the line.) We ended on the last plate's side.",
      cleric: "Poor old Grubnak, burnt, and still so keen to serve us. Four plates, one for each of us, lit in turn. We stood on our own, stepped off the line of fire, and finished on the last plate's side. I was proud of the order we kept.",
      grovekeeper: "The old cook came up out of the fire like a charred stump, and set four plates, one each, lit in order, a burning furrow from each to the next. I stood on mine, stepped out of the furrow, and ended where the last plate stood.",
      chronomancer: "Grubnak, burnt, and the old kitchen. Four plates, lit in the order the rims fill, a line of fire from each to the next. I stood on mine as it lit and stepped off as the line came. It will have been neatly done.",
      tidecaller: "Four plates rose like rocks in a falling tide, one for each of us, each lit in turn, a line of fire running from one to the next. I stood on mine, stepped off the line, and waited on the side where the last plate stood.",
      lanternkeeper: "The old cook came back burnt, and set four plates round the hall. I stood on mine, watched the fire run from it to the next, and stepped off. Ended on the side where the last plate stood. Four plates, four of us.",
      kenseier: "Grubnak, burnt, and still keeping good time. Four plates in an order I could count, a line of fire from each to the next. I stood on mine till it fell, stepped through the line, and ended on the last plate's side. Honest, for a cook.",
      houndmaster: "Old cook's back, singed, with four covered plates and a fire running from each to the next. Stood on mine till it was served, off the line after, then to the last plate's half. The lads smelt dinner.",
    } },
    'Burning Web': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Three threads of fire ran wall to wall and cut the hall in pieces, a knot on each. I broke the nearest and watched its thread burn out from the knot toward both walls, so I stood off that road. A thread left whole went up all at once.",
      gloomsworn: "Her web, burnt: three threads of fire, wall to wall, a knot on each. Break a knot and the thread burns out from it, both ways, like a fuse, so I stood clear of the fuse. A thread left whole goes up with her shadow. I know the dark that follows a thread.",
      thresher: "The old Broodmother's web, scorched, dear: three burning threads and a knot on each. Break a knot and the thread burns out from it, both ways. Break them all, and stand where no fire is running. A thread left whole goes up all at once.",
      bloodreaver: "The spider's web, on fire! Three threads, a knot on each, and when I broke one the thread burned out from it like a fuse. Where you cut decides where the fire goes, my friend. I broke each and stepped off the road it ran. Late cutters get burnt.",
      runeguard: "Three threads of fire, wall to wall, a knot on each. Break a knot and its thread burns out toward both walls, so choose the cut by where the fire will run. A thread left whole goes up at once.",
      gorgemaw: "Skitterra's web, flambéed. A handsome starter: three threads, a knot on each. Break one and it burns out toward both walls like a fuse, so I broke each where the fire would run away from me. A thread left whole is a dish gone hot. Pretty, and gone in a bite.",
      oathbroker: "Section: three threads, wall to wall, a knot on each to be broken. A broken knot burns its thread outward toward both walls, and a thread left whole goes up entire. We broke all three and stood clear of the road.",
      ironpalm: "She spoke in threads, burnt. Three lines wall to wall, a knot in each. When I broke a knot, the thread burned outward from it, both ways, and I stepped off the road. A thread left whole speaks all at once.",
      ranger: "Three burning threads wall to wall, a knot on each. Break a knot and the fire runs out from it, both ways. Break all three, stand off the line the fire takes. A thread left whole goes up at once.",
      shadowblade: "The old spider's web, scorched: three threads and a knot on each. I broke one and the fire ran out from it like a fuse, so I stood clear. Her shadow raced along any thread left whole, which is showy.",
      pyromancer: "The web is ON FIRE, and I could not be happier! Three threads, a knot on each. I broke one and the flame ran out of it toward both walls, so I stood off the road and cast from there. A thread left whole goes up at once.",
      thermist: "Three threads, a knot on each. Break a knot and the thread burns outward, both ways. Step off the road. A thread left whole goes up all at once, her shadow racing along it, and that is the one that scalds.",
      driftblade: "Three threads of fire across the hall and a knot on each, and I broke the nearest and the thread burned out from it both ways, and I slipped through the gap beside it and round, and the ones left whole went up behind me all at once.",
      alembist: "Three burning threads, a knot on each. Break a knot and its thread burns outward toward both walls, so cut where the fire runs away from us. A thread left whole goes up at once, so break all of them.",
      threadweaver: "Three threads of fire, wall to wall, and I know a bad seam when I see one. Break the knot and the thread burns outward from it, both ways, like a fuse. I picked each cut so the fire ran away from us. A thread left whole goes up entire.",
      marionettist: "The old Broodmother's web, scorched: three burning ropes across the stage, a knot in each. Break the knot, and the rope burns out from it. (Exit the company from the line of fire.) A rope left whole goes up in one flash. We cut every one.",
      cleric: "Skitterra, burnt, and her web alight: three threads and a knot on each. I told everyone to break each knot and step off the road the fire ran, outward toward both walls. A thread left whole goes up all at once.",
      grovekeeper: "Skitterra's web, alight like dry grass: three threads, a knot on each. Break a knot and the fire runs out from it both ways, like a fuse through stubble. I broke each where the burning would run away from my lot.",
      chronomancer: "The Broodmother's web, burnt. Three threads, a knot on each, and I broke them in turn. A broken knot sends the fire along its thread toward both walls, so I broke it away from where I stood, or will have; it comes to the same. A thread left whole goes up at once.",
      tidecaller: "Her web, burnt: three threads and a knot on each, like three tide lines drawn wall to wall. Break a knot and the fire runs out from it, both ways. We broke each and stood where the burning was not. A thread left whole goes up at once.",
      lanternkeeper: "Three threads of fire, wall to wall, a knot on each. I broke one and watched the fire run out toward both walls, so I lit the unburnt ground and stood there, and the rest came to me. A thread left whole goes up all at once.",
      kenseier: "Skitterra, burnt: three threads of fire, a knot on each. The stroke is the break: the fire runs out from the knot toward both walls like a fuse, on a count I could keep. We cut all three and stood off the road.",
      houndmaster: "Old spider's web, singed: three threads of fire, a knot on each. Broke one, fire ran out of it toward both walls, so I had the dogs off the road. A thread left whole goes up in one go. Sit. Every knot broken in the end.",
    } },
    'Ember and Ash': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Volkar, burnt: each of us was given a charge, fire or ash, until the act ended. Opposites drifted together, the same kind pushed apart, gently, always. The surge fell on pairs, so I stood with my opposite. The drift went on through the next moves.",
      gloomsworn: "The storm titan, burnt, and each of us marked fire or ash. Opposites were drawn together, the same kind pushed apart, all the time. The surge landed on pairs, so I stood by my opposite. The pull lasted until the act ended.",
      thresher: "Volkar, scorched, dear, and each of us made fire or ash. Opposites drift together, the same kind drift apart, the whole act. The surge falls on pairs, so stand by your opposite. Do mind the drift in everything after.",
      bloodreaver: "The storm one, burnt, and he marked us fire or ash like a butcher marking cuts! Opposites pulled together, the same pushed apart, the whole act. The surge fell on pairs, so I stood by my opposite and took it side by side.",
      runeguard: "A sign on each of us, fire or ash, recorded here for my successor: opposites are drawn together and like kinds pushed apart until the act ends. The surge strikes pairs, so each stands beside an opposite. The drift persists through all that follows.",
      gorgemaw: "Volkar, grilled, and each of us seasoned fire or ash, so that opposites drew together and the same kind pushed apart. The surge fell on pairs, a dish meant for two. I stood by my opposite and ate it with them.",
      oathbroker: "The Storm Titan, burnt, and a charge assigned to each: fire or ash, until the act is concluded. Opposites are drawn together, like kinds repelled. The surge lands on pairs, so I stood by my opposite and held against the drift.",
      ironpalm: "Volkar spoke in two words, fire and ash, and each of us was given one. The opposite word drew us together, the same word pushed us apart, the whole act. The surge was said to a pair. I stood by my opposite.",
      ranger: "Volkar's echo. Each of us marked fire or ash. Opposites drift together, same kind drift apart, till the act ends. Surge lands on pairs: stood with my opposite. The drift carried on through the rest of the act.",
      shadowblade: "The storm titan, scorched, and each of us marked, fire or ash. Opposites drift together, like kinds apart, and the surge falls on pairs. I stood with my opposite, which was a nuisance, since I like to stand alone. It saw us pair, and I let it.",
      pyromancer: "FIRE and ASH, and I was fire, naturally! Opposites drift together and the same kind pushes apart, all act, and the surge fell on pairs. I stood by my opposite and cast from there. The drift never let up.",
      thermist: "I was ash. Fire is drawn to me, and ash pushes away, steadily, until the act ends. The surge lands on pairs, so I stood by my opposite and let the drift carry me, through the rest of the act too.",
      driftblade: "Volkar made each of us fire or ash, opposites drifting together and the same kind apart, the first time a floor has ever moved me, and I let it, and found my opposite for the surge, and the drift carried on through every move after.",
      alembist: "Each of us was given a charge, fire or ash, until the act ends. Opposites are drawn together, so let them, and stand paired with your opposite when the surge lands. The drift lasts through everything after, so plan with it.",
      threadweaver: "Volkar, burnt, and a thread tied to each of us, fire or ash. Opposites pulled together, like kinds pushed apart, all act. The surge fell on pairs, so I tied my stitches in twos, opposite to opposite. The drift went on after.",
      marionettist: "Volkar, scorched, and the company cast as fire or ash: opposites pulled into duets, like kinds blown apart. The surge fell on pairs. (A pas de deux for each of us.) The drift carried on through every scene after.",
      cleric: "Volkar, burnt, and each of us made fire or ash. Opposites were drawn together, like kinds pushed apart, all the act. The surge fell on pairs, so I put each beside their opposite. The drift stayed with us.",
      grovekeeper: "Volkar's weather, burnt: each of us marked fire or ash, and opposites pulled together while like kinds pushed apart, like warm and cold air. The surge fell on pairs, so I stood by my opposite. I read the drift through everything after.",
      chronomancer: "Volkar, burnt, and each of us given a charge, fire or ash, till the act's end. Opposites drift together, the same drift apart. The surge falls on pairs, so I paired early, and will again. The drift ran on through every move after.",
      tidecaller: "A charge came down on each of us, fire or ash, and a current took hold: unlike drifted together, like drifted apart, and it ran the whole act. The surge broke on pairs, so I let the current carry me to my opposite.",
      lanternkeeper: "Volkar's echo gave each of us a charge, fire or ash, until the act ended. Opposites drifted together, like kinds apart. The surge fell on pairs, so I stood beside my opposite and hung a light on us both. The drift stayed all act.",
      kenseier: "Volkar, burnt, and each of us marked, fire or ash. Opposites drift together, like kinds apart, on a pull the blade cannot turn. The surge falls on pairs. I stood by my opposite and let the drift do the work. It lasted the whole act.",
      houndmaster: "Volkar's back, singed. Gave each of us fire or ash. Opposites drift together, same kind drift apart, the whole act. Surge falls on pairs, so I stood by my opposite. Rust and Ash kept apart on their own.",
    } },
    'The Crier': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The Hollow King rang his bell, and Doom fell on one of the others, with a lantern lit from a halo flame. Whoever stood nearest walked over it and carried it to the doomed. His knights then swept the Sovereign's last half again, so the line stayed off it.",
      gloomsworn: "The old king rang for the next call and laid his doom on one of us. A lantern lit from the halo, to be walked over and carried to the doomed. His knights then repeated the Sovereign's last sweep, the same half. I stayed off it. I have been the echo before.",
      thresher: "The King rings his bell, dear, and one of us is given his doom, and a lantern is lit from the halo. Whoever is nearest takes it to the doomed. Then his knights sweep the same half the Sovereign just swept. Do mind where you stand.",
      bloodreaver: "The old bones rang a bell and doomed one of us, and a lantern lit off the halo! Walk over it, carry it to the doomed, and no one dies. Then his knights swept the half the Sovereign had just swept, and I knew better than to go back to it. Good manners.",
      runeguard: "The bell, then a doom on one of us, and a lantern lit from the halo. Someone takes the lantern up and carries it to the doomed. His knights then replay the Sovereign's last sweep, on the same half; I did not return to it.",
      gorgemaw: "The old King rang for the next course and doomed one of my friends, with a lantern lit off the halo as the antidote. Someone must walk it over. His knights then sent the last dish back out, the very same half. I was not hungry for it a second time.",
      oathbroker: "Notice by bell. A doom is entered against one of us, and a lantern lit from the halo is the surety: one of us must carry it to the doomed. The knights then repeat the last sweep on the same half. We stayed out.",
      ironpalm: "He rang a bell and spoke a doom over one of us, and lit a lantern from the halo. I took it up and carried it to the doomed. Then his knights said the Sovereign's last word again, on the same half. I did not return to it.",
      ranger: "Bell. Doom on one of us, lantern lit off the halo. Picked it up, carried it over to the doomed. Knights then repeated the Sovereign's last sweep, same half. Stayed out of it.",
      shadowblade: "The old King rang his bell and doomed one of us, and lit a lantern off the halo. I carried it across, unseen, which the doomed found alarming. His knights repeated the Sovereign's last sweep, the same half, as if it needed saying twice.",
      pyromancer: "A bell, and doom on one of us, and a lantern lit from the halo, the fire that saves and the fire that burns! I carried it to the doomed and stayed clear of the rest. His knights then swept the same half again, and I was already elsewhere.",
      thermist: "Bell, doom on one of us, a lantern lit from the halo. Walk it to them. I ran it over and arrived scalding. Then the knights replayed the Sovereign's last sweep on the same half, and I was long gone from it.",
      driftblade: "He rang the bell and doomed one of us and lit a lantern off the halo, and I took it up and ran it through the room to the doomed, and the knights swept the same half the Sovereign just had, and I was already through and out of it.",
      alembist: "Doom on one of us, ticking. A lantern lit off the halo, walked over and carried to the doomed in time. The knights then repeat the Sovereign's last sweep on the same half, so do not go back.",
      threadweaver: "The bell, then a doom on one of my stitches and a lantern lit from the halo. I carried it across to the doomed before the thread was cut. His knights then sewed the Sovereign's last sweep over the same half, and I kept my stitches well off that patch.",
      marionettist: "The old King rang a bell, doomed one of the company, and lit a lantern off the halo. (A prop, to be carried to the doomed before the curtain.) His knights then reprised the Sovereign's last sweep on the same half. We kept to the other side.",
      cleric: "He rang his bell and laid doom on one of our friends, with a lantern lit from the halo. I took it up myself and carried it to them. His knights then swept the same half the Sovereign had just swept, and we kept off it.",
      grovekeeper: "The old King rang a bell like church before weather, and a blight fell on one of my lot, and a lantern lit off the halo. I carried the cure over. His knights then swept the same half again, like thunder after lightning.",
      chronomancer: "A bell, a doom on one of us, a lantern lit from the halo, which I carried over to the doomed. Then the knights replayed the Sovereign's last sweep on the same half, an echo late, and I was somewhere else.",
      tidecaller: "The bell rang like a buoy, and a doom settled on one of us like a slow tide, and a lantern lit off the halo. I carried it over before the water rose. His knights then swept the same half again, like surf off a wall.",
      lanternkeeper: "A bell, a doom on one of us, a lantern lit from a halo flame. I walked over it, picked it up, and carried it to the doomed before the wicks burned low. His knights then swept the Sovereign's last half again. I kept to the lit side.",
      kenseier: "The old King rang his bell, and doom fell on one of us, and a lantern lit off the halo. A blade cannot turn a doom, so I carried the light over. His knights then replayed the Sovereign's last sweep on the same half. I stayed off it. He gave notice first.",
      houndmaster: "Bell, then doom on one of ours, and a lantern lit off the halo. Walked over it, took it up, carried it across to the doomed. Knights swept the Sovereign's last half again after. Dogs stayed off it.",
    } },
    'Royal Doom': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Doom fell on one of the line, and only the lantern could lift it. I held my ground and let the bearer through. Later it passed from one to the next, each glowing before it landed, so the bearer walked to them early.",
      gloomsworn: "Doom, laid on one of us, and the lantern the only cure. The dead do not hurry; the bearer must. Later it passed from one to another, each glowing before the doom reached them, and the lantern went on with the cure. Walk early. The dead can wait; the living cannot.",
      thresher: "Doom on one of us, dear, and the lantern is the cure! It passes along: whoever is next glows a little before it lands, so the lantern goes to them early, and the cured one carries it on. Do hurry.",
      bloodreaver: "Doom on one of us, my friend, and the lantern the only cure! Later it passed from one to the next, each glowing before it landed, and the lantern went along. Get there early, cure them, and no one dies. Barely a scratch.",
      runeguard: "Doom falls on one of us, and the next is seen to glow before it lands. The lantern goes to the glow early, cures them, and passes on with the cure. The order held every round.",
      gorgemaw: "Doom, a poisoned course for one of my friends, and the lantern the only antidote. Later the plate passed down the table, each guest glowing before it landed, and the lantern went along with the cure. Carry it early. Never leave a guest waiting.",
      oathbroker: "Doom entered against one of us, and the lantern is the only discharge. It passes to the next party, who glows before it falls; the bearer attends them early, and the lantern goes on with the cure. The order is fixed.",
      ironpalm: "Doom fell on one of us, and the lantern was the cure. Later it passed along, each one glowing before it landed, and the lantern passed with the cure. I walked to the glow early, calm, and waited.",
      ranger: "Doom on one of us, lantern the only cure. It passes on: next one glows before it lands. Carry the light to the glow early, cure, hand on. Same order each round.",
      shadowblade: "Doom on one of us, and the lantern the cure. It passed on, each one glowing before it landed, so I took the light to them early and unseen, which the doomed found alarming.",
      pyromancer: "Doom on one of us, and a lantern as the cure, my own kind of fire! It passed from one to the next, each glowing before it landed, so I ran the light to them early and carried it on.",
      thermist: "Doom on one of us, the lantern the only cure. It passed on, the next one glowing before it landed, so I ran the light to them early. Cure, carry on.",
      driftblade: "Doom on one of us and the lantern the cure, and when it passed on, each one glowing before it landed, I was already running the light over to the glow, curing them, and on to the next, early every time.",
      alembist: "Doom, passing from one of us to the next, and the lantern the only cure, brought to each in time. The next one glows before it lands, so go early, cure, and carry the lantern on. The order never changes.",
      threadweaver: "Doom on one of my threads, and the lantern the only needle. It passed from one to the next, each glowing before it landed, so I drew the light over early and carried it on with the cure.",
      marionettist: "Doom on one of the company, and the lantern the only cure. (A prop, passed along.) Each next player glowed before the doom reached them, so the light went to the glow early and on with the cure. The order never changed.",
      cleric: "Doom on one of us, and the lantern the only cure. I took it to them, and later it passed from one to the next, each glowing before it landed, so I walked to the glow early and carried on.",
      grovekeeper: "A blight on one of my lot, and the lantern the only cure. It passed from one to the next, each glowing before it landed, so I carried the cure to the glow early, and on. The order never changed.",
      chronomancer: "A doom on one of us, and the lantern the only cure, which I carried over. It passed on in the same order each round, the next one glowing before it landed, so I was early.",
      tidecaller: "Doom settled on one of us like a slow tide, and the lantern was the only shore. It passed from one to the next, each glowing before it landed, so I carried the light to them early.",
      lanternkeeper: "Doom on one of us, and the lantern the only cure. It passed on, each one glowing before it landed, so I carried the light to them early, and on with the cure. The order never changed.",
      kenseier: "Doom fell on one of us, and no blade turns a doom. The lantern does. It passed on, each one glowing before it landed, so I carried the light to the glow early, and on.",
      houndmaster: "Doom on one of ours, lantern the only cure. Passed on, next one glowing before it landed, so took the light over early, cured them, carried on. Same order each round, dogs clear.",
    } },
    'Royal Tithe': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Everyone was brought low at once, down to the last of our strength, and the healer had little time. I kept the line close beside them and the ground steady, and mended what I could.",
      gloomsworn: "The tithe took all of us at once, down to the last of ourselves. I know what it is to be that low. I kept to the healer's side, and stayed up, which is more than most can say after that.",
      thresher: "The tithe took all of us, dear, down to the last thread at once, and every name was suddenly very close to the front of the list. So we stayed near our healers, who had work to do. Nobody's name reached the front.",
      bloodreaver: "Everyone knocked down to the last scrap of themselves in one blow! I could feel it, and I loved it, and the others did not. I stayed up and kept close to the healer. A deep one, and a long way from healed.",
      runeguard: "The bell sounds, and the court's own account is called in. Everyone is brought down to the last of their strength, and the healer must mend all before the next toll. Stay close, and stay still.",
      gorgemaw: "A very small portion left on every plate, and every guest brought low at once. I was not unhappy, since I had it before. I stayed close to the healer, and ate what was given. Underseasoned, and mean.",
      oathbroker: "The royal tithe: all that is owed, collected from every one of us at once, to the last third of what we had. I stayed close to the healer, who settled the balance before the next call. So noted, with resentment.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke the tithe, and every one of us was brought low at once. I did not answer. I stood near the healer, still, and breathed, and let the mending come. Then the toll came.",
      ranger: "Everyone down to a sliver. Stayed close to the healer, kept still, waited for the mending. No line out of it. Held my ground.",
      shadowblade: "It took the tithe from all of us at once, which is vulgar. I stayed close to the healer, as did everyone, and kept very still. I was glad of the company, just then.",
      pyromancer: "Everyone brought down to the last of themselves, and I, who never go quietly, went quietly! I stayed close to the healer and held my tongue and my spells. It nearly killed me, the hush.",
      thermist: "All of us brought low at once, down to the last of our heat. Close to the healer, quickly, and still. I wanted to run, and did not. Stay near the one mending.",
      driftblade: "Everyone dropped to the last of themselves at once and I was told to stand still, which is the one thing I hate, so I stood close to the healer and did not run, and the mending came, and then I was off.",
      alembist: "Everyone brought down to the last of their strength at once. Stay close, keep still, let the healers brew the cure, and have it ready before the toll. Nothing on my shelf covers this one, the whole party at once.",
      threadweaver: "The tithe took the whole hem down to the last thread at once. I tied my stitches close and kept them still, and mended as fast as I could. There was very little slack in it.",
      marionettist: "The tithe called, and the whole company was brought low in one beat. (Hold position, downstage, near the healer.) We stood very still, until the mending came. A grim second act.",
      cleric: "The tithe took all of us down to the last of our strength at once. I called them in close, and prayed, and mended each of them before the toll. The others stayed still, bless them.",
      grovekeeper: "The tithe came like a hard frost, and every one of my lot was cut to the root at once. I gathered them in, and planted the mending as fast as I could, before the toll. Plant fast.",
      chronomancer: "The tithe took all of us at once, down to the last of ourselves, and I had very little time to mend. I gathered them close and kept time, and nobody had to be undone. Nearly nobody.",
      tidecaller: "The tithe drew us all down at once, as if the sea had gone out too far, to bare rock. I raised the water as fast as I could, before the toll, and everyone stayed close. There was a hurry, for once.",
      lanternkeeper: "The tithe put out the lights on all of us at once, down to the last glow. I walked around and lit each one again, and told them to stand close so I could reach. Four wicks, trimmed and lit.",
      kenseier: "The tithe came, and a blade cannot turn it, and every one of us was brought down at once. We drew close to the healer and stood very still, and let the mending come before the toll.",
      houndmaster: "Every one of us knocked down to a scrap, dogs and all. Kept still and close by the healer, and waited. They mended us before the toll.",
    } },
    'Dirge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The bell tolled for the Dirge, after the tithe, a short time after, and whoever was not mended in time was lost. I kept near the healer and stood steady until the mending was done.",
      gloomsworn: "The Dirge tolled after the tithe, and anyone who was still low did not rise from it. I stayed close to the healer and waited to be mended. I was dead for the last one. I did not care for the second.",
      thresher: "The Dirge tolls a little after the tithe, dear, and anyone not mended by then is called. So we stayed near our healers, and let them work, and made sure of our own names.",
      bloodreaver: "The Dirge tolled a while after the tithe, and anyone who wasn't mended by then went down! I made sure the healer had me, and stood close, and took the toll with a full cup. A good one, my friend. Not for the weak.",
      runeguard: "The tithe, then a pause, then the Dirge; the sequence was read in advance. Be mended in the pause, every one of us, before the toll. Stay close to the healer and do not wander.",
      gorgemaw: "The tithe was the first course, and the Dirge the last: a sombre bell some time later. Anyone who had not eaten, been mended, did not get dessert. I made certain of my portion, and stayed near the healer. Thin, but final.",
      oathbroker: "The Dirge falls due a short while after the tithe, and any not brought back to strength by then default. We stayed close to the healer and settled before the toll. Use the gap between.",
      ironpalm: "The tithe was a word, and the Dirge a long sentence after. Whoever was not mended by its end was not heard again. I stayed close to the healer and breathed, and we all answered.",
      ranger: "Dirge tolls a short while after the tithe. Anyone not mended by then is down. Stayed near the healer, waited for the mending, took the toll whole.",
      shadowblade: "The Dirge tolls a short time after the tithe, and anyone not mended is lost, which is a cruel sort of manners. I stayed near the healer and was mended, like everyone. The toll came and went without finding me.",
      pyromancer: "The Dirge tolled a short time after the tithe, and anyone not mended by then went out like a candle! I stayed close to the healer and let myself be mended, and waited for the bell. Fully mended, and rather proud of it.",
      thermist: "The Dirge tolls a short time after the tithe, and anyone still low is lost. I felt every heartbeat in the gap. Stayed near the healer, mended, whole, and the toll passed over.",
      driftblade: "The Dirge tolled a little after the tithe and anyone still low was gone, so I stood still, which I hate, near the healer, and was mended in the gap, and then the toll passed through us and I could run again.",
      alembist: "The Dirge tolls a short time after the tithe, and anyone not mended falls. Everyone close to the healer, mended in the gap before the toll. The cure must be brewed ahead: tithe, mend, toll.",
      threadweaver: "The Dirge tolled a short time after the tithe, and any stitch not mended by then snapped. I kept them all close and mended them one at a time, before the toll. There was still a little give, just.",
      marionettist: "The Dirge, a short time after the tithe: the last bell of the act. (Everyone mended before the curtain, or struck from the playbill.) The company stayed close to the healer, and took the toll whole. A grim finale, but a tidy one.",
      cleric: "The Dirge tolled a short time after the tithe, and anyone not mended by then would have fallen. I went to each of them, in turn, and made sure. Every one of them stood still for it.",
      grovekeeper: "The Dirge tolled a short while after the tithe, like a late frost, and any of my lot not tended by then would not have grown again. I tended each one, as fast as I could, before the toll. Tend fast.",
      chronomancer: "The Dirge tolls a little after the tithe, and anyone not mended in the gap is undone. I mended everyone in the gap, quickly, and mostly in order. Mostly, as I will have said.",
      tidecaller: "The Dirge tolled a little after the tithe, like the last wave of a series, and anyone not raised by then did not rise. I raised each of us in the gap, and the toll broke on a full shore.",
      lanternkeeper: "The Dirge tolled a short time after the tithe, and anyone whose light was still low went out. I went round with my pole and lit each again in the gap, and counted the lamps. Four of us, four glowing.",
      kenseier: "The tithe was the first beat, and the Dirge the last, a toll after a rest. A blade cannot turn it, so we used the rest: stayed close, got mended, stood through the toll.",
      houndmaster: "Dirge tolls a bit after the tithe, and anyone not mended is gone. Had the dogs tight by the healer, waited, got mended in the gap. Took the toll whole.",
    } },
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------- Act II's echoes (Hrimgar's Glass Floor, the Hydra's Heads of Flame, Vhalgrath's Cinder Dive)
    'Glass Floor': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Hrimgar stamped once at the rim and a crack ran out from his foot, sheet by sheet, each painted before it broke. I took the line to unpainted ground and held there, and crossed the broken sheets only after the crack had passed. The black water stays.",
      gloomsworn: "The old giant of ice came up red as a forge and stamped, and the floor split outward from his heel. I walked past the last painted sheet and waited. Black water is no place to stand, living or dead, so I crossed it and kept walking.",
      thresher: "Hrimgar stamped, dear, and the floor broke outward from his foot one sheet after another, each painted first. We stood on the unpainted ground, or stepped onto broken sheets once the crack had gone by, and never lingered in the black water.",
      bloodreaver: "The big frozen one came up glowing and STAMPED, my friend, and a crack raced out from his heel! I ran for the unpainted floor, let it pass, then waded over quickly. The black water bites and drags if you stay. A bite I was glad to miss.",
      runeguard: "His foot lifts, and every sheet the crack will reach is painted at once. Be off a sheet before the crack arrives, onto unpainted ground or ground already broken; the black water that remains is crossed, never stood in.",
      gorgemaw: "Hrimgar, red-hot glass for a glaze! He stamped, and the floor cracked outward like a sugar crust, sheet by sheet. I kept to the unpainted ground, then crossed the broken sheets briskly, for the water beneath will not let a man linger.",
      oathbroker: "Hrimgar's clause: every painted sheet is forfeit as the crack reaches it, nearest his foot first. We moved to unpainted ground or onto sheets already broken, and crossed the black water without lingering, since it keeps its interest.",
      ironpalm: "He stamped, and the crack travelled outward, sheet by sheet, not all at once. I stepped to a place the paint did not reach, waited, then crossed the broken sheets without stopping. Black water likes a still foot.",
      ranger: "Hrimgar stamps, crack runs out from his heel. Every sheet it will reach is painted at once. Went past the painted edge, held, crossed the broken sheets once the crack was by. Black water slows whoever stays.",
      shadowblade: "He stamped, the crack ran out from his heel sheet by sheet, and I was already on ground it would not reach. Broken sheets fill with black water, so I crossed them once and did not stop.",
      pyromancer: "Hrimgar, RED, and glowing, which suits a giant, though I will thank him not to compete. He stamped and the floor cracked outward, sheet by sheet. I stood past the painted edge and cast through it, then crossed the broken glass quickly.",
      thermist: "The glass was red hot to look at. It breaks outward from his heel, every sheet painted first. Stepped past the painted edge, crossed the broken sheets without stopping, never stood in the black water.",
      driftblade: "He stamped, and the crack went out from his heel sheet by sheet, and I was moving before the paint finished, out to ground it would not reach, then through the broken sheets and the black water without once standing still, which is the only way across.",
      alembist: "The floor, red hot, breaking outward from the giant's heel. Stand past the painted edge, or step onto the broken sheets once the crack has passed. The black water left behind is crossed, not lingered in. I would not brew in it.",
      threadweaver: "He stamped, and the seam ran out from his heel, sheet by sheet, each painted before it split. I kept my stitches on unpainted ground and crossed the split sheets only once the seam had passed them. The black water drags at a slow foot.",
      marionettist: "Our giant stamped (the floor, red hot, splitting outward from his heel, sheet by sheet). We took our marks past the painted edge, and crossed the broken sheets after the crack had gone by. (Do not linger in the black water. The Doll nearly did.)",
      cleric: "Poor Hrimgar, burning now, and the floor cracking outward from his heel, one sheet after another. I kept everyone on unpainted ground and crossed the broken sheets only after the crack had passed. The black water hurts whoever stays in it. The paint was a kindness.",
      grovekeeper: "The floor went red and cracked outward from the giant's heel like a field in drought, sheet after sheet. I stood where it would not reach, and crossed the broken ground only when the crack had passed. Black water drowns roots.",
      chronomancer: "I knew what he would do: a stamp, and a crack running out from his heel, each sheet painted first. I stood past the painted edge, and crossed the broken sheets after the crack had passed. I did not stand in the black water. I knew better.",
      tidecaller: "Hrimgar stamped, and the floor broke outward from his heel like ice in spring, one sheet after another, with black water welling behind. I stayed past the painted edge and crossed the water without lingering. The tide takes whoever is slow.",
      lanternkeeper: "The old giant, red as a lantern, stamped and a crack ran out from his heel, sheet by sheet. I stood past the painted edge, and crossed the broken sheets only when the crack had gone by. Black water does not hold a light.",
      kenseier: "One stamp, then the crack running out from his heel in a measured beat, sheet by sheet, each painted first. I stood past the painted edge, and crossed the broken sheets once the crack had gone by. Black water slows a still foot.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad Hrimgar, glowing red, stamped, and a crack ran out from his foot one sheet at a time. Had the dogs past the painted edge, crossed the broken sheets after, never stood in the black water. Rust hates a wet floor.",
    } },
    'Heads of Flame': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A head of fire rose from the floor and began biting whoever it chose. A ring followed its mark, then locked. I cut the head down first, kept the line clear of whoever wore the ring, and stayed off the stump's fire.",
      gloomsworn: "A head of fire came up out of the floor and chose its meat at random: a ring followed one of us, then locked. The wearer walked clear of the rest and stepped out as it locked. We cut the head down. The stump burned on, and I stayed off it.",
      thresher: "A head of fire rose up out of the floor, dear, and picked one of us to bite, a ring following them about before it locked. The marked one went off alone and stepped out as it fell. We cut the head down quickly, and kept off its burning stump.",
      bloodreaver: "A head of fire, my friend, rising out of the floor and snapping at whoever it fancied! The ring followed me once, then locked, so I walked it away from the others and stepped out. Then we cut the head down. The stump still burns. Stay out of it.",
      runeguard: "A floor sigil lights, and a head of fire stands from it and bites at random, a ring following its mark before it locks. The marked one leads the ring from the others and steps out at the lock; the head is cut down; the stump is left alone.",
      gorgemaw: "The Hydra's answer: a head of fire, rising from the floor like a pudding on the boil, snapping at whomever it fancied. The ring followed one of us, then locked. I led it away from the others, stepped out, and helped carve the head down. The stump smoulders.",
      oathbroker: "A head of fire stood from its sigil and bit at whoever the lot chose, a ring following the party in question before it locked. The named party walked it clear of the others and stepped out. We struck the head down at once. Its stump burns, and holds a lien.",
      ironpalm: "A head of fire rose and chose one of us to bite, a ring following until it locked. I walked the ring away from the others, stepped out at the last, and we struck the head down. I did not step into the stump's fire.",
      ranger: "Head of fire off a floor sigil, biting at random. Ring follows its mark, then locks. Took mine out away from the others, stepped clear at the lock. Cut the head down fast. Stump burns on; stayed off it.",
      shadowblade: "The head rose from the floor and picked someone to bite, the ring following them until it locked. Mine, once. I took it away from the others and stepped out at the lock, then helped cut the head down. The stump burns on.",
      pyromancer: "A head of FIRE! Out of the floor, biting at whoever it liked, a ring following its mark and then locking. I led mine away from the others and stepped out at the end. We cut it down, and its stump kept burning in my honour.",
      thermist: "A head, rising from a sigil. It bites at random, a ring following its mark and locking. Led mine away from the others and stepped out. Cut the head fast. Stayed out of the stump's fire.",
      driftblade: "A head of fire stood up out of the floor and chose one of us at random, the ring following me and I ran with it, away from the others, and out at the last moment as it locked, then round, and in with the blade to cut the head, and clear of its stump.",
      alembist: "A head of fire, rising from a floor sigil, biting at random, a ring following its mark and then locking. Lead the ring away from the others, step out at the lock, cut the head down. The stump burns on, and nobody should stand in it.",
      threadweaver: "A head of fire rose out of the floor and pulled at one of us, a ring on the one chosen, following before it locked. I drew mine away from the others and stepped out at the end of it. We cut the head through. Mind the burning stump, and the slack.",
      marionettist: "A head of fire rose from the floor and bit at a hero of its choosing (the ring follows, then locks). The chosen one took their mark away from the company and stepped out at the last. We cut the head down. (The stump burned on. The Doll kept off.)",
      cleric: "Hydra's head of fire rose from the floor, biting at whom it would, a ring following them before it locked. The one marked led it away from the others and stepped out. We cut the head down. I mended the bitten.",
      grovekeeper: "A head of fire grew up out of the floor like a weed with a mouth, and bit at whoever it chose, a ring following them and locking. Mine walked clear of the others and stepped out. We cut the head at the root. The stump smoulders yet.",
      chronomancer: "A head of fire rises from a sigil, and bites at random, a ring following its mark before it locks. I led mine away from the others and stepped out at the lock. I cut the head down early, and kept off the stump's fire.",
      tidecaller: "Out of the floor came a head of fire like a reef at low tide, and it bit at one of us, the ring trailing its mark before it locked. The marked one led it off from the rest and stepped aside at the end. We cut the head down. Keep off the stump.",
      lanternkeeper: "A head of fire rose from the floor, brighter than any lamp of mine, and bit at one of us, a ring following before it locked. I led mine away from the others and stepped out at the end. We cut the head down. The stump burns.",
      kenseier: "A head of fire, rising from the floor and biting in a slow beat at one hero of its choosing, a ring following, then locking. I led mine away from the others and stepped out as it locked. We cut the head down. The stump burns on.",
      houndmaster: "A head of fire came up out of the floor and picked one of us to bite, a ring following until it locked. Took mine away from the dogs and the others, and stepped out at the end. Cut the head down quick. Stump burns; Rust won't go near it.",
    } },
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------- the Sovereign's own count (Act II): rings over every head, and a tower for each in turn
    'The Anvil Count': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Rings hung over every head, and one went out with each tower struck. When a single ring was left on me, a tower rose in my colour across the floor. I went round the black water, stood in it, and took the blow for the rest.",
      gloomsworn: "Gold rings over each of us, one fewer after every tower. When mine was the last, a tower rose in my colour on the far side of the room. I crossed dry floor where I could and stood in it. Another's tower is not mine to take. The dead do not queue.",
      thresher: "Little rings over every head, dear, and an anvil struck for each in turn. When mine was down to one, a tower came up in my colour right across the room. Round the black water I went, and into it.",
      bloodreaver: "Rings over every head, my friend, and an anvil rung for each, one at a time! When mine was the last, a tower rose in my colour clear across the floor, and I ran for it round the black water and struck it square. Glorious.",
      runeguard: "Rings over every head, and one fewer as each tower is struck. When one ring remains, a tower rises in your own colour on the far side of the floor; you stand in it, and no other's.",
      gorgemaw: "The Sovereign laid a table of anvils, and every guest was given rings, one fewer with each course. At my last, my course rose in my colour at the far end of the hall. I crossed round the black water and swallowed it whole.",
      oathbroker: "The clause: each of us holds rings, and one is struck from every hand as each tower falls. On the last ring, a tower rises in your colour across the floor, and yours alone is yours to stand in. A wrong tower is a debt on everyone.",
      ironpalm: "Rings over every head, and one fewer after each blow of the anvil. When mine was the last, a tower rose in my colour across the floor. I crossed without hurry, kept out of the black water, and stood still within it.",
      ranger: "Rings over each head. One goes out per tower struck. Last ring on me: tower rises in my colour, far side of the floor. Took the dry route round the black water, stood in it. Wrong tower, or none, hurts all of us.",
      shadowblade: "Rings over each of us, and one winked out with every tower. When mine was the last, a tower rose in my colour across the room. I slipped round the black water to it and took the blow for everyone.",
      pyromancer: "Rings over every head, and mine, naturally, the brightest! One fewer after each tower, and when I had one left my own tower rose across the floor in my colour. I ran round the black water and stood in it like a candle.",
      thermist: "Rings over every head, one fewer per tower struck. On the last ring, a tower rises in your colour across the floor. Went round the black water, stood in it, and left the rest alone.",
      driftblade: "The rings went out one by one as the towers were struck, and when mine was the last a tower rose across the floor in my colour, and I was already running, round the black water and in, and the blow was mine and the floor was still.",
      alembist: "Rings over every head, one fewer after each tower struck. On your last ring, stand in the tower of your colour across the floor, going round the black water. Another's tower is not yours to take.",
      threadweaver: "Rings over every head, like stitches counted off, one fewer for every tower struck. At my last, a tower rose in my colour across the floor, and I drew myself over to it round the black water. One thread, one tower.",
      marionettist: "Rings over every head (the order of entrances), one fewer after each blow of the anvil. On my last, a tower rose in my colour across the stage, and I took my mark in it. (The Doll went the dry way round.) A tidy cue.",
      cleric: "Rings over each of us, and one fewer as every tower was struck. When I was down to my last, mine rose in my colour across the floor, and I went to it round the black water and took the blow. I mended after.",
      grovekeeper: "Rings over every head, shed one by one like petals as each tower was struck. At my last, a tower rose in my colour across the floor, and I walked to it round the black water and took root there.",
      chronomancer: "I knew the order before it began: rings over every head, one fewer for each tower. When I had one left, my tower rose across the floor in my colour, and I was already crossing, round the black water, as I had been all along.",
      tidecaller: "Every head wore a ring, and every struck tower washed one away. My last ring called up a tower in my own colour, far across the floor. I waded round the black water, took my place in the shallows of it, and left the others' towers alone.",
      lanternkeeper: "A ring of light over each head, one put out for every tower struck. When mine was the last lit, a tower rose in my colour across the floor, and I carried my lamp there round the black water and stood in it.",
      kenseier: "Rings over every head, one fewer on each beat of the anvil. On my last, a tower rose in my colour across the floor, and I crossed on the beat, round the black water, and stood in it.",
      houndmaster: "Rings over every head, the dogs' too, near enough. One fewer after each tower. When I was down to one, a tower rose in my colour across the floor. Walked the dogs round the black water and stood in it.",
    } },
    'Cinder Dive': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Vhalgrath marked a scorched spot, then laid a line of embers to it from beyond the rim, and dived. I stayed off both, then went into the middle of where he landed, the one quiet place when the ground rang out. Steam followed where the line crossed black water.",
      gloomsworn: "The tyrant marked a spot, and a line of embers to it from beyond the rim, and fell along it. I stood clear of both, and after the landing went into the heart of where he struck, where the ringing ground does not reach. Then I stepped out of the steam.",
      thresher: "Vhalgrath, dear, marked a spot, then a road of embers to it, and down he came. We kept off both, and once he had landed went into the quiet middle of the ring, then out of the steam that rose where the road crossed the black water.",
      bloodreaver: "The big winged one marked a spot, laid a line of embers to it, and dived, my friend! I stayed off the spot and the line, then ran into the very heart of the landing, where the ringing ground cannot reach. Then I dodged the steam. A scar I am glad to miss.",
      runeguard: "A spot painted first, then a line of embers to it from past the rim. Stand clear of both, and after the landing go into the quiet middle of the ring, then out of the steam bursting where the line crossed the black water.",
      gorgemaw: "Vhalgrath, a dragon on a plate! He marked a spot, laid a line of embers to it, and fell down it like a dropped dish. I kept off both, then went into the quiet middle of the ringing ground, and out of the steam.",
      oathbroker: "Vhalgrath named a spot, then a line of embers to it, and fell upon it. We stayed clear of both, and entered the quiet middle after he landed, since the ring that follows spares only that. Then out of the steam where the line had crossed the water.",
      ironpalm: "He chose a spot, then a line of embers to it, and fell the length of it. I kept from both. After the landing I walked to the quiet middle, where the ringing ground could not follow, and left it again when the steam rose.",
      ranger: "Spot painted first, then a line of embers from past the rim. Tyrant dives it. Stood clear of both, went into the middle after he landed. Ring runs out, middle stays quiet. Steam bursts where the line crossed black water; stepped out.",
      shadowblade: "He marked a spot, laid a line of embers to it, and fell. I was clear of both, and into the quiet middle of the landing as the ground rang out, then away from the steam. The middle is the one place a falling thing forgets.",
      pyromancer: "Vhalgrath, FIRE on a line, diving! A spot painted first, then a road of embers to it. I stood clear of both, then into the quiet middle of the landing as the ground rang out. The steam I stepped out of was only for show, and I wish someone had seen.",
      thermist: "A spot painted, a line of embers to it, a dive. Then the ground rings out, except the middle. Stood clear of both, went to the middle after the landing, stepped out of the steam. Into the middle. Literally.",
      driftblade: "A spot painted, and then a line of embers laid to it from past the rim, and the tyrant fell down it, and I was clear of both, then in toward the quiet middle as the ground rang out, and out again through the steam before it burst.",
      alembist: "A winged tyrant diving along a line of embers onto a painted spot. Stand clear of the line and the spot, enter the quiet middle after the landing, step out of the steam. It bursts where the line crossed the black water, and it boils.",
      threadweaver: "A spot marked, a line of embers drawn to it from past the rim, and Vhalgrath fell down it like a plumb line. We stayed off the line and the spot, then went into the quiet middle after he landed, and clear of the steam.",
      marionettist: "Vhalgrath (our tyrant, winged) marked his spot, then laid a line of embers to it, and fell. We stayed clear of both, and took the quiet middle after the landing as the ground rang out. (Then out of the steam. The Doll was damp.)",
      cleric: "Vhalgrath, a spot marked, a line of embers laid to it, and down he came. I kept everyone off the line and the spot, then led them into the quiet middle after the landing, and out of the steam. A little middle was all we needed.",
      grovekeeper: "The tyrant marked a spot, laid a line of embers to it from past the rim, and dived like a hawk. We kept off both, then went into the quiet middle after the landing, where the shaking ground cannot reach, and out of the steam.",
      chronomancer: "A spot painted, a line of embers laid to it, a dive. I stood clear of both, then went into the quiet middle just after the landing, as the ground rang out. Steam where the line crossed black water; I stepped out.",
      tidecaller: "Vhalgrath marked a spot, laid a line of embers to it, and fell as a gannet falls. We stayed off both, then went into the quiet middle after the landing, where the ringing ground does not reach, and out of the steam. Stillness, in the heart of it.",
      lanternkeeper: "A spot marked, then a line of embers to it, and the tyrant fell along it like a dropped lamp. I stood clear of both, then went into the quiet middle after he landed, and out of the steam. Keep out of the line.",
      kenseier: "A spot marked, a line of embers to it, then the dive: a rhythm I could count. I stood clear of both, took the quiet middle after the landing as the ground rang out, and stepped out of the steam.",
      houndmaster: "Big winged lad marked a spot, laid a line of embers to it, and dived. Dogs off both, then into the quiet middle after the landing, where the ringing ground can't reach, and out of the steam.",
    } },
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------- Azerath's Twin Brands, and the wall at the act's middle
    'Twin Brands': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Azerath branded the two nearest the middle, and a wall lit. Frost or lightning, the pair met in the blue circle; fire or void, they fled to opposite corners. The wall changed its mind once. Late on, our charges decided it: opposite met, matching fled. I kept clear.",
      gloomsworn: "Two of the living, nearest the middle, took Azerath's brands, and a wall lit. Frost or lightning, the pair came together at it; fire or void, they left for opposite corners. The wall changed its mind once. Late on, the charges we carried decided it: opposite came together, matching left. I stayed out.",
      thresher: "Azerath branded the two nearest the middle, dear, and lit a wall. Frost or lightning wanted the pair together in the blue; fire or void wanted them in opposite corners. The wall can change once. Late on, the charges we carried said it first: opposite meet, matching part. The rest kept clear.",
      bloodreaver: "Two brands, my friend, on whoever stood nearest the middle, and a wall glowing! Frost or lightning, the pair marched to the wall and stood together; fire or void, they ran to opposite corners. The wall changes once. Late on, our charges chose: opposite met, matching ran. I stayed clear.",
      runeguard: "Two nearest the middle are branded, and a wall is lit. Frost or lightning: the pair converges in the blue circle; fire or void: they depart to opposite corners. The wall changes once. Later the charges carried were the sign: opposite converge, matching depart; the rest keep clear.",
      gorgemaw: "Azerath seasoned the two nearest the middle and lit a wall. Frost or lightning, the pair gathered in the blue like guests at one table; fire or void, they fled to opposite corners. The wall changed its order once. Late on, our charges set the table: opposite sat together, matching fled.",
      oathbroker: "Azerath's clause: the two nearest the middle are branded, one wall lit. Under frost or lightning both stand in the blue circle; under fire or void both leave for opposite corners. The wall may be amended once mid-cast. Later, the charges held govern: opposite unite, matching part. Others keep clear.",
      ironpalm: "The two nearest the middle were branded, and a wall lit. Frost or lightning asked the pair to come together in the circle; fire or void asked them to leave for opposite corners. The wall can change once. Later our charges answered it: opposite came together, matching left. I kept clear.",
      ranger: "Two nearest the middle get branded, one wall lit. Frost, lightning: the pair goes in together, in the blue. Fire, void: out, far corners, apart. Wall can swap once; wait for the final paint. Later the charges decide: opposite meet, matching split. Rest of us stay off the wall.",
      shadowblade: "The two nearest the middle were branded, and one wall lit. Frost or lightning, the pair met in the blue; fire or void, they parted for opposite corners. The wall changed once, so I let the paint settle. Later it asked our charges instead: opposite meet, matching part. I stayed off.",
      pyromancer: "Two brands on whoever stood nearest the middle, and a wall lit in an ELEMENT, and once it was fire, which I took personally! Fire or void, the pair fled to opposite corners; frost or lightning, they met in the blue. It changes once. Late on our charges decided: opposite met, matching fled.",
      thermist: "Frost or lightning: the two branded, nearest the middle, meet in the blue circle. Fire or void: they run to opposite corners. The wall can change once; I waited for the paint. Late on, charges decided it: opposite meet, matching run.",
      driftblade: "Two brands on the pair nearest the middle, and a lit wall, and I was off already, then stopped, because the wall changed once: frost or lightning, the pair meets; fire or void, they split to opposite corners. Later our charges said it first: opposite meet, matching split. Wait, then go.",
      alembist: "Two branded, nearest the middle, one wall lit. Frost or lightning, bring the pair together in the blue circle; fire or void, part them into opposite corners. The wall may change once; later the charges carried decide it: opposite together, matching apart. Observe before dosing.",
      threadweaver: "Two brands, like two threads through one needle, on the pair nearest the middle, and a wall lit. Frost or lightning drew the pair together in the blue; fire or void drew them to opposite corners. The wall can change once. Late on, our charges drew it: opposite together, matching apart.",
      marionettist: "The two nearest the middle were branded (our cue: a wall lit). Frost or lightning, the pair takes the blue circle together; fire or void, they exit to opposite corners. The wall may change once. Later the company's charges set the scene: opposite meet, matching exit. (The Doll waited.)",
      cleric: "Azerath branded the two of us nearest the middle, and lit a wall. For frost or lightning, the pair came together in the blue; for fire or void, they ran to opposite corners. The wall changes once. Later our charges said it: opposite met, matching ran.",
      grovekeeper: "Two brands took hold like grafts on the pair nearest the middle, and a wall woke. Frost or lightning, they went to it and stood together in the blue; fire or void, they left for opposite corners. One change is allowed. Late on, our charges decided: opposite met, matching left.",
      chronomancer: "The rule came to me from the tower. Two nearest the middle branded, one wall lit: frost or lightning, the pair must meet in the blue; fire or void, they part to opposite corners. The wall turns once, so I waited out the turn. Late on the charges decide: opposite meets, matching parts.",
      tidecaller: "Two brands on the pair nearest the middle, and a wall lit like a beacon. Frost or lightning brought the pair together in the blue; fire or void sent them to opposite shores. The light can shift once. Later our charges set the tide: opposite met, matching parted.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two of us nearest the middle were branded, and one wall lit like a lamp in a window. Frost or lightning, the pair met in the blue circle; fire or void, they left for opposite corners. The lit wall can change once. Later our own charges decided it: opposite met, matching left.",
      kenseier: "Two brands on the pair nearest the middle, and a wall lit: two readings, no more. Frost or lightning, the pair meets in the blue; fire or void, they split to opposite corners. The wall changes once. Later our charges named the reading: opposite meet, matching split.",
      houndmaster: "Two lads nearest the middle got branded, and one wall lit. Frost, lightning: the pair ran to it and stood together in the blue. Fire, void: far corners. The wall can swap once, so we waited for the paint. Late on, our charges decided: opposite met, matching split. Dogs well off.",
    } },
    'The Heart Remembers': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Sealed. The Sovereign could not be touched, and the heads we had cut rose again from their stumps, first-cut first, each ringed on the floor before it burned. I gave each its ground. After the last came a held breath, then the blade's half, then the blast, and the line closed up.",
      gloomsworn: "The Sovereign went still and shut, and the heads we cut answered from where they fell, in the order they died, each pillar ringed beforehand. A head left living answered last. I stood out of every ring as it showed. A hush, the blade, the blast, and the seal broke like glass.",
      thresher: "The Sovereign was sealed, dear, and every head we had cut came back for a second helping, in the order I crossed them off! Each pillar was painted first, so we stepped out of the paint. A hush after the last, then the blade's half, then close to our healers for the blast.",
      bloodreaver: "Sealed up tight, the big one, and the heads we cut stood up out of their stumps in fire, in the order we cut them, my friend! Every pillar had its ring painted first. After the last, a hush, then the blade, then the healer's side for the blast.",
      runeguard: "The seal closes and the stumps burn in the order the heads fell, each ring painted before its pillar, any living head last. Leave each ring as it appears; after the hush, leave the blade's half; close upon the healer for the blast.",
      gorgemaw: "The Sovereign sealed the pantry, and the heads we had carved came back for second helpings, each a column of fire in the order carved, its plate painted first. I stepped off every plate. After the last, a hush, then the blade's half to avoid, then close to the healer for the blast.",
      oathbroker: "The Sovereign was sealed, and the cut heads called in what we owed them, each stump a pillar in the order of cutting, its ring painted ahead; a head still living answered last. We vacated each ring in turn. After the hush: the blade's half, then the healer's side for the blast.",
      ironpalm: "The Sovereign could not be touched, and the heads we had cut spoke again, one after another, each a pillar of fire from its stump, each ring shown first. I let each pass without hurry. After the last, a hush, the blade's half, then the healer for the blast.",
      ranger: "Sealed. No hitting it. Cut heads burn up again from their stumps, same order they fell; a live head goes last. Each ring painted first. Out of the ring, next ring. Hush after the last, then the blade's half, then tight on the healer for the blast.",
      shadowblade: "It was sealed against us, so I watched the stumps. They burned up one by one in the order we cut the heads, each ring painted a moment before. When the last went quiet, the blade came, then the blast, and I was by the healer.",
      pyromancer: "Sealed! Not one spell could reach the Sovereign, so the stumps took the stage, flaring into pillars in the order we cut the heads, each ring painted first. I stepped out of every one. The hush after the last was the cue for the blade's half, then the blast. A fine show.",
      thermist: "Sealed, nothing to hit. Then the stumps flare one at a time, in the order the heads fell, each ring painted ahead. Out of each ring as it showed. A hush after the last, then the blade's half, then in tight by the healer for the blast.",
      driftblade: "Sealed, and nothing to cut but the air, so I moved, and the stumps lit one after another in the order we cut the heads, each ring painted ahead of its flame, and I was out of each, then through the hush, out of the blade's half, and in by the healer.",
      alembist: "A sealed Sovereign. The cut heads erupt from their stumps in the order cut, each ring painted first, a living head last. Leave each ring as it shows; after the hush, leave the blade's half; stand close to the healer for the blast. Sequence matters.",
      threadweaver: "The seal drew shut, and the stumps of the cut heads went up in fire in the order they were cut, like stitches pulled along a seam, each ring marked first. I stepped out of every one. After the hush, out of the blade's half, then in to the healer.",
      marionettist: "The Sovereign was sealed (house lights down), and each head we had cut made its entrance again from its stump, in order of exit, rings painted first. We left each mark in turn. After the hush, the blade's half, then we huddled by the healer for the blast. (The Doll bowed.)",
      cleric: "Sealed in, the poor thing, and our cut heads came back to burn from their stumps in the order we cut them, each ring painted first. I walked everyone out of every ring as it showed. After the hush, away from the blade's half, then close to me for the blast.",
      grovekeeper: "The Sovereign sealed itself like a closed bud, and the stumps we left behind burst into fire one by one, in the order we cut, each ring marked first. I stepped out of each. After the hush, out of the blade's half, then in close to the healer for the blast.",
      chronomancer: "Sealed, and I remembered the order the heads fell: the stumps burn in that order, each ring painted first, a living head last. So I left each ring on time. After the hush, the blade's half, then close to the healer for the blast. I had it all by heart, being the one who counts.",
      tidecaller: "The Sovereign sealed, and the stumps of the heads we had cut flared one after another, in the order of the cutting, each ring marked first, like waves coming back up a beach. I stepped out of each. After the hush, out of the blade's half, then close to the healer for the blast.",
      lanternkeeper: "The Sovereign sealed itself, and the stumps we left lit one after another, in the order we cut the heads, each ring painted first, like lamps along a road. I stepped out of every ring. After the hush, out of the blade's half, then in by the healer for the blast.",
      kenseier: "Sealed, and the stumps struck in the order the heads fell, a count I could keep, each ring painted first. I left each ring on its beat. The hush after the last was the rest in the bar, then the blade's half, then the healer for the blast.",
      houndmaster: "Sovereign sealed shut, nothing to bite. The stumps flared in the order we'd cut the heads, each ring painted first. Dogs out of every ring. After the hush, out of the blade's half, then in by the healer for the blast. Rust sat the last one out.",
    } },
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------- the landing's ring (Cinder Dive's own cast) and the turn into the second form
    'Aftershock': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "After the landing the ground rang out across the whole floor, ringed everywhere but the quiet middle, where he had struck. I took the line in there at once, and stood. The ring passed over the rest.",
      gloomsworn: "Where the tyrant landed, the ground rang out across the whole hall, all but the heart of it, which stayed quiet. I went into the heart and waited, as I wait in any grave. The ringing passed me by.",
      thresher: "The ground rang out after the landing, dear, over the whole floor but the quiet middle where he struck. So in we went, all of us, snug as a coffin, and let it pass. Do mind your hem.",
      bloodreaver: "He landed, and the ground RANG, my friend, the whole floor but the quiet middle where he hit! I went in there, which felt like cheating, and let the ring go over. Fine by me.",
      runeguard: "A landing, and the ground drawn out in a ring across the whole floor, its middle left unmarked. Enter the unmarked middle, where he struck, before the ring goes out, and remain. It passes over the rest.",
      gorgemaw: "After the landing, the ground rang out like a dinner gong across the whole hall, save the quiet middle where he struck, which is the one place with no plate laid. We took our seats there and let it pass.",
      oathbroker: "Following the landing, the ground rings out over the whole floor, excepting the quiet middle where he struck. That exemption is the whole of the clause. We entered it before the ring and remained until it had passed.",
      ironpalm: "The ground rang out after the landing, everywhere but the quiet middle where he struck. I walked into the middle without hurry and stood still while it passed. The quiet is real.",
      ranger: "Landing, then the ground rings out. Whole floor ringed but the middle, where he hit. In before it goes, stand still, it passes over. Anywhere else, it finds you.",
      shadowblade: "The ground rang out after he landed, over the whole floor but the middle, where he struck. Nobody watches the place a thing fell from. I stood in it, and the ring passed over me.",
      pyromancer: "He LANDED and the ground rang out across the hall like a struck bell, FIRE in every board of it except the quiet middle where he hit! I stood there, grinning, and it passed over me.",
      thermist: "The landing, and the ring marked across the whole floor. It leaves a quiet middle where he struck. Stood in the middle, let the ring pass. As ever.",
      driftblade: "After the landing the ground rang out across the hall and I was already into the quiet middle where he struck, which is the one still place, and I stood in it, and the ringing went over me like wind over a stone.",
      alembist: "The ground rings out across the whole floor after a landing. The quiet middle, where he struck, is untouched: enter before the ring goes and remain. Nowhere else is safe. The eye of it.",
      threadweaver: "The ground rang out after the landing, a ring drawn across the whole floor like a seam, with the middle left unstitched where he struck. I took us into the unstitched part and let the seam run past.",
      marionettist: "After the landing, the ground rang out across the whole stage, all but the quiet middle where he struck (the one spot the spotlight skips). We took our marks there and let it pass over us. (The Doll was spared.)",
      cleric: "The floor rang out after the landing, everywhere but the quiet middle where he struck. I led them into the middle and held them there until the ringing had passed. Such a quiet place to be, in the midst of all that.",
      grovekeeper: "After the landing the ground rang out across the whole hall like a field shaken by a storm, but not in the quiet middle where he struck. We rooted there, close together, and let it pass.",
      chronomancer: "A landing, then the ground rings out over the whole floor except the quiet middle where he struck. I went into the middle in good time and stood still while it passed. I had stood there already, in my head.",
      tidecaller: "The tyrant landed and the ground rang out in a ring across the whole hall, like a wave off a dropped stone, but the middle where he struck lay calm. The eye is calm. We stood in it, and it passed over us.",
      lanternkeeper: "After the landing the ground rang out over the whole floor, and the only safe place was the quiet middle where he struck, like a dark corner of a lit room. I stood in it, and the ring went by.",
      kenseier: "A landing, then the ground rang out in a ring across the whole floor, one blow, no counting it, but for the quiet middle where he struck. I took the middle before it went, stood still, and let the ring pass.",
      houndmaster: "He landed, and the ground rang out across the whole floor, bar the quiet middle where he'd struck. Took the dogs in there and held them still till it passed. Rust didn't like it, but he stayed.",
    } },
    'Rebirth': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "It drove the blade into the floor and every fire in the hall ran into it. Dark, no sound; the ring opened wide as a wheel of blades. Nothing struck us, and nothing of ours would touch it. At the end came the blast. I closed up by the healer.",
      gloomsworn: "It drove the blade into the floor and drew every fire in the hall down into it, and the dark came, and the quiet. I have kept watch in worse. The ring opened into a wheel, the sky split, and no blow came until the last, one blast. I stayed close to the healer.",
      thresher: "It stuck its blade in the floor, dear, and every fire in the hall ran down it like water! Dark and quiet, then the ring of flames opened into a great wheel, and the sky split. Nothing hurt us until the very end, when the blast came. We gathered close to our healers.",
      bloodreaver: "It rammed the blade in the floor and sucked every fire in the room down it, my friend! Dark, silent, nothing hitting me, which I did not enjoy. Then the ring opened wide and the sky tore open. The blast at the end made up for it. I stood by the healer.",
      runeguard: "The blade is driven into the floor, and every fire in the hall drawn after it into the dark. Patience, for nothing strikes in the dark, nor can it be struck; the ring opens, the sky parts, and the blast lands last, on all of us. Close upon the healer.",
      gorgemaw: "It plunged the blade into the floor like a fork into a roast, and every fire in the kitchen ran into it! Dark, and not a sound. The ring opened into a wheel of blades, the sky split. No bite came until the blast at the end, so I stood by the healer.",
      oathbroker: "The Sovereign drove its blade into the floor, and every fire in the hall was drawn in, to dark and silence. The ring of flames opened into a wheel; the sky split. Nothing struck us, nor could we strike. At the close, a blast on all parties. We stood by the healer.",
      ironpalm: "It drove the blade into the floor and every fire in the hall followed it. Dark, and quiet. Then the ring opened into a wheel, and the sky split. Nothing struck us, and nothing of ours reached it. I waited, and at the end the blast came, and I stood close to the healer.",
      ranger: "Blade into the floor. Every fire in the hall drawn after it. Dark, no sound, nothing hitting. Ring opens wide, sky splits. Can't hit it, so I waited. Blast comes at the end, all of us. Tight on the healer.",
      shadowblade: "It drove the blade down and drank every fire in the hall, and the dark was mine at last: no sound, and nothing struck us. Then the ring opened into a wheel and the sky split, and everyone could see. The blast came at the end. I was by the healer.",
      pyromancer: "It stuck its blade in the floor and every flame in the room went RUNNING into it! I wept a little, at the waste. Dark, quiet, nothing struck us, nothing of mine reached it, then the ring opened wide, the sky split, and the blast hit everyone at the end. Healer's side.",
      thermist: "Blade in the floor, every fire in the hall pulled after it, then dark and quiet. The ring opens into a wheel and the sky splits, and the blast comes after, on all of us. Nothing struck us in between. Stayed by the healer.",
      driftblade: "The blade went into the floor and every fire in the hall ran after it, and the dark came, and no wind at all, which I hated, and then the ring opened wide and the sky split and nothing touched us until the blast at the very end, and I was in by the healer.",
      alembist: "A blade in the floor, and every fire in the hall drawn into it. Then dark and quiet, then the ring opening into a wheel, the sky splitting. Nothing struck us, nor could we strike it. Close upon the healer before the blast at the end. Patience.",
      threadweaver: "It drove the blade down like a needle, and every fire in the hall ran after it like a thread pulled through, and the room went dark and still. The ring opened into a wheel and the sky split. Nothing struck us. The blast came last; I stood by the healer.",
      marionettist: "It drove the blade into the stage and every light in the house ran down it. Blackout, silence (our cue to wait). Then the ring opened into a great wheel, and the sky split like a backdrop. Nothing struck us until the last bar, the blast. We stood by the healer. (The Doll held still.)",
      cleric: "The blade went down into the floor and every fire in the hall followed it, leaving a dark and a silence that frightened me a little. Nothing struck us. Then the ring opened and the sky split, and the blast came last for all of us. I kept everyone near me through it.",
      grovekeeper: "It drove the blade into the floor like a spade, and every fire in the hall ran down into the ground like sap in autumn. Dark and quiet, and nothing struck us. Then the ring opened wide, and the sky split. The blast came at the end. We gathered by the healer.",
      chronomancer: "The blade went into the floor, every fire drawn after it, the hall dark and still. Nothing struck us, nor could we strike it; I waited. The ring opened, the sky split, and the blast came at the end, as I'd supposed. I stood by the healer for it, and will again.",
      tidecaller: "It drove the blade into the floor and every fire in the hall ebbed away into it, and the room lay dark and still. The ring opened into a wheel and the sky split. Nothing struck us until the surge at the end, the blast. We stood by the healer.",
      lanternkeeper: "It drove the blade into the floor and every fire in the hall drained away into it, my lamps with them, and the room went dark. Then the ring opened into a wheel, and the sky split, and our light came back. Nothing struck us until the blast. Close to the healer.",
      kenseier: "The blade into the floor, every fire in the hall drawn after it, then a long rest in the bar: dark, quiet, nothing struck us. The ring opened into a wheel, the sky split, and the blast fell on the last beat, on all of us. I stood by the healer.",
      houndmaster: "Blade went into the floor, every fire in the hall ran after it, and the dark came down. Rust whined, so I kept him close and the others tight. Nothing hit us and nothing of ours reached it. Ring opened wide, sky split, blast came last. By the healer.",
    } },
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------- Act III: Corvane, Caelum Vey and Brannoc
    'Crossed Blades': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Corvane knelt at its side, and before each unleashing he took a stance that named it: blade low for Crescent, blade drawn for Lance, blade sheathed for Pyre. Then both of them struck, his half the mirror of its. I read his stance and set my feet before the cast began.",
      gloomsworn: "Corvane knelt beside it, and I knew the posture; I have knelt so myself. Before each unleashing he showed which one: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Then both blades did it. The dead are reliable about their forms.",
      thresher: "Corvane knelt beside it, dear, and before every unleashing he took a stance to say which: blade low for Crescent, drawn for Lance, sheathed for Pyre. Then both of them did it, his half turned about. Such a courteous warning.",
      bloodreaver: "Corvane knelt by it, my friend, and before each blow he showed us which: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre! Then both of them swung, his half the mirror of its. Two scars for the price of one. I read him and braced.",
      runeguard: "Corvane kneels at its side and takes a stance before each unleashing. Blade low foretells Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Ready yourself for the named one, and expect it twice, his half mirrored.",
      gorgemaw: "Corvane knelt at my host's elbow like a sommelier, and before each course he showed which was coming: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Then both kitchens served it at once, his half mirrored. Always read the menu.",
      oathbroker: "Corvane knelt beside the counterparty, and before each unleashing he gave notice of its form: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Both then performed it, his half mirrored, as though sealed by both. Notice was given, and we read it.",
      ironpalm: "Corvane knelt at its side. Before each blow he spoke without words: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Then both spoke it at once, his half a mirror of the other. I listened first. Then I moved.",
      ranger: "Corvane kneeling at its flank. Before every unleashing, a stance: blade low, Crescent; drawn, Lance; sheathed, Pyre. Then both blades, his half mirrored. Read the stance, pick the ground before the cast starts.",
      shadowblade: "Corvane knelt at its side and told us everything in advance, which is the opposite of my trade. Before each unleashing he took a stance: blade low, Crescent; drawn, Lance; sheathed, Pyre. Then both blades did it, his half turned round. I chose my corner early.",
      pyromancer: "Corvane knelt beside him, and before each unleashing he struck a pose, a good one, I'll admit: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre! Then both of them performed it, his half reversed. Read the pose, choose your ground.",
      thermist: "Corvane kneels, and takes a stance before each unleashing. Blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Then both strike at once, his half mirrored, and I ran at it too early, too eager. Wait for the stance.",
      driftblade: "Corvane knelt at its side and before each unleashing took a stance, blade low for Crescent, drawn for Lance, sheathed for Pyre, and then both of them went at it at once, his half the mirror of its, so I read the stance first and picked my way through before the cast even began.",
      alembist: "Two blades at once, one a mirror of the other. Corvane kneels beside it and takes a stance before each unleashing: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Read the stance first, then choose the ground. Never dose before the label.",
      threadweaver: "Corvane knelt at its side, and before each unleashing he took a stance to tell us which seam it would cut: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Then both blades ran at once, his half stitched in reverse. Read it early.",
      marionettist: "Corvane knelt at the star's side (a lovely bit of staging) and before each unleashing took a stance that told the house which was next: blade low, Crescent; drawn, Lance; sheathed, Pyre. Then both performed it together, his half mirrored. (I take my cue early. The Doll takes it earlier.)",
      cleric: "Corvane knelt at its side, poor man, and before each unleashing he took a stance to tell us which: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Then both blades struck together, his half turned about. I read his stance and called everyone to their places.",
      grovekeeper: "Corvane knelt at its side and read the coming weather for us: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Then both blades struck, his half turned the other way, like wind from two quarters. Read the sky before it breaks.",
      chronomancer: "Corvane kneels, and before each unleashing he takes a stance that names it: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Then both blades play the same figure, his half a mirror. I read him early and moved. I knew all along, which is not quite the same as learning.",
      tidecaller: "Corvane knelt at its side like a rock the tide goes round, and before each unleashing his stance named the wave to come: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Then both blades broke at once, his half mirrored. I read him, and waited.",
      lanternkeeper: "Corvane knelt beside it, and before each unleashing he took a stance, like a lamp turned toward the corner it will light: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Then both blades struck, his half mirrored. I picked my corner first.",
      kenseier: "Corvane knelt at its side, and before each unleashing he took a stance that gave the count away: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Then both blades struck on the same beat, his half mirrored. An honest courtesy.",
      houndmaster: "Corvane knelt at the big lad's side, and before each unleashing he took a stance to say which: blade low, Crescent; blade drawn, Lance; blade sheathed, Pyre. Then both of them did it, his half turned round. Sat the dogs by me and read him first.",
    } },
    'Crescent': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two great fans of the blade, its own on one of us and Corvane's a quarter round from it, landing together, and then both turned a little further. One wedge of floor stayed clear of all four. I found it before the first and kept my feet in it.",
      gloomsworn: "Two fans of fire, its own and Corvane's, a quarter apart, and then both swung a little further. Between the four there is one wedge no blade reaches. I went to it and stayed. I have kept still in worse places.",
      thresher: "Two broad fans, dear, its own and Corvane's a quarter round, landing together, and then both turned a little further. There was one wedge the blades never touched, in either beat. I skipped into it before the first and stayed put. Such tidy steps.",
      bloodreaver: "Two huge fans of the blade, my friend, its own and Corvane's a quarter round, landing together, then both swinging further round! One wedge of floor stayed untouched through both, and I stood in it. I declined the wound, this once.",
      runeguard: "Two broad fans, its own at one of us and Corvane's a quarter round from it, together, and then both turned further. A single wedge lies clear of all four edges across both swings. Stand in it before the first, and do not wander.",
      gorgemaw: "Two wide slices, its own and Corvane's a quarter round, served together, then both carved a little further round. One wedge of the platter stayed clean through both courses. I sat in it before the first was brought. Overdone, and easily avoided.",
      oathbroker: "Two fans, its own upon one of us and Corvane's a quarter round from it, due together, then both amended a little further round. One wedge is exempt from both. I took up residence in it before the first fell.",
      ironpalm: "Two great sweeps, its own and Corvane's, a quarter round apart, spoken together and then again, turned. One wedge of floor heard neither. I stood in it before the first word and did not move between them.",
      ranger: "Two fans of steel, its own and Corvane's a quarter round off, then both swung round further. One wedge clear of all four, both beats. Found it before the first, held it, never shifted.",
      shadowblade: "Two big, showy fans, its own and Corvane's a quarter round from it, landing together, and then both turning a little further, as if one were not enough. One wedge the blades never saw, in either beat. I slipped into it early and stayed.",
      pyromancer: "Two great fans of steel and flame, its own and Corvane's a quarter round off, then BOTH swung round further! Nothing wrong with the show. One wedge of floor stayed clear through the whole thing, so I stood in it and cast on.",
      thermist: "Two fans, its own and Corvane's a quarter round off, landing together. Then both swing further, and nothing is safe unless you stood in the right wedge from the start. One wedge stayed clear of all four. Stand there. Don't swing.",
      driftblade: "Two fans, its own and Corvane's a quarter round from it, and then both swung further round, and I wanted to run through all of it, and could not, because one clear wedge stayed clear through both beats, and the only way through was to go there early and be still.",
      alembist: "Two wide fans, its own and Corvane's a quarter round off, landing together, then both turned further. One wedge of floor stays clear through both beats. Stand in it before the first, and do not wander. Wide, but never complete.",
      threadweaver: "Two fans of cloth, I thought, cut wide: its own and Corvane's a quarter round from it, together, then both turned further. One wedge stayed uncut through both. I put myself in it before the first and drew my stitches in beside me.",
      marionettist: "Two sweeping fans (its own, and Corvane's a quarter round, a mirror image downstage), landing together, then both turned further. There is one wedge of stage the blades never reach. I took my mark in it before the cue, and held the blocking. (No moving between beats.)",
      cleric: "Two great fans of the blade, its own and Corvane's a quarter round from it, landing together, and then both turned further. One wedge of floor stayed clear through both beats. I called everyone into it before the first, and held them there. Such a small gap, and so welcome.",
      grovekeeper: "Two gales from two quarters, its own and Corvane's a quarter round, and then both turned further, like a wind that changes. One wedge stayed calm through both. I moved into it before the first and held there, my lot beside me.",
      chronomancer: "Two fans at once, its own and Corvane's a quarter round off, and then a second count with both turned further. One wedge of floor stays clear on both. I stood in it before the first swing, and was still standing there for the echo.",
      tidecaller: "Two great waves of the blade, its own and Corvane's a quarter round from it, breaking together, and then both again, turned further. One wedge of calm water stayed clear through both. I went to it before the first and let the swell go by.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two fans, its own and Corvane's a quarter round off, together, then both turned further round. One wedge of floor stayed clear through both. I walked into it before the first and stayed, one lamp lit at my feet. Four of us, one wedge, just room enough.",
      kenseier: "Two fans, its own and Corvane's a quarter round off, on the same beat; then both turned further on the next. One wedge of floor was clear on both. I stood in it before the first beat and did not step between them. Honest, if wide.",
      houndmaster: "Two big fans, its own and Corvane's a quarter round off, then both swung round further. One wedge clear of all four, both times. Whistled the dogs in and sat them in it before the first. Didn't shift between.",
    } },
    'Lance': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A dash straight through whoever stood farthest from it, out to the wall, and a beat later Corvane's through whoever stood farthest from him, never the same one. Each line is aimed once and does not follow. Whoever was marked sidestepped it. I kept off both.",
      gloomsworn: "It ran straight through the farthest of us to the wall, and Corvane, a beat later, through the farthest from him, never the same one. Each line was drawn once and did not follow. The one it chose stepped off. I have been singled out before.",
      thresher: "It dashed through whoever stood farthest, dear, out to the wall, and a beat later Corvane did the same through the one farthest from him, never the same guest twice. The line is laid once and does not follow, so the chosen simply stepped aside.",
      bloodreaver: "It charged through whoever stood farthest from it, out to the wall, and Corvane a beat later through whoever stood farthest from him, never the same one! Each line is aimed once and never follows. I kept in close, so it skipped me, which was insulting. Whoever was picked stepped off the line.",
      runeguard: "Two lances in sequence: the first runs from the Sovereign to the wall through the one standing farthest off, the second from Corvane through the one farthest from him, and the same person is never taken twice. Neither line bends once drawn. The chosen sidesteps; the rest keep out of both.",
      gorgemaw: "A skewer through the farthest guest, my host to the wall, and a beat later Corvane's through whoever was farthest from him, never the same one. Each is aimed once and does not follow, so the one chosen simply leaned aside. The rest of us kept off both. Two skewers and no sauce between them.",
      oathbroker: "The clause: it dashes through the party farthest from it, to the wall; a beat later Corvane through the one farthest from him, never the same party twice. Each is served once and does not follow, so the named party steps aside, and the rest keep off both lines.",
      ironpalm: "It ran through the farthest of us to the wall. A beat later, Corvane ran through the farthest from him, never the same one. Each line is drawn once and does not follow. The chosen stepped aside; the rest stayed off both. Two questions, two clear answers.",
      ranger: "Dash through whoever stands farthest from it, out to the wall. Corvane a beat later, through the farthest from him, never the same one. Lines laid once, no tracking. The chosen steps off the line; everyone else keeps out of both.",
      shadowblade: "It dashed through whoever stood farthest from it, to the wall, and Corvane a beat later through whoever stood farthest from him, never the same one. The lines are aimed once and never follow, so the chosen simply stepped out of the way. I stayed off both.",
      pyromancer: "It dashed through whoever stood farthest, out to the wall, and Corvane a beat later through whoever stood farthest from him, never the same person! Each line is aimed once and never follows. I was chosen, naturally, and sidestepped with great dignity. The rest kept off both.",
      thermist: "It dashes through the farthest of us to the wall, then Corvane through the farthest from him, never the same one. Lines aimed once, no following. I was picked and stepped aside, breathless. Everyone else kept clear of both lines.",
      driftblade: "It ran through whoever stood farthest, out to the wall, and a beat later Corvane ran through whoever stood farthest from him, never the same one, and each line was aimed once and never followed, so the chosen slipped aside, which I did, and loved, and the rest stayed clear.",
      alembist: "A dash through the farthest of us to the wall, then Corvane's through the farthest from him, never the same one. Each line is aimed once and does not follow, so the chosen steps aside, and everyone else stays clear of both. Marked, thankfully, in red.",
      threadweaver: "It ran a thread through the farthest of us, out to the wall, and Corvane ran his a beat later through the farthest from him, never the same one. Each is pulled once and does not follow, so the chosen simply stepped aside. The rest kept clear of both.",
      marionettist: "A dash through whoever stood farthest upstage, out to the wall, and a beat later Corvane's through whoever stood farthest from him, never the same actor. Each line is blocked once and never follows, so the chosen stepped out of it, and the company stayed off both. (Exit, pursued by nothing.)",
      cleric: "It ran through the farthest of us to the wall, and Corvane, a beat later, through the farthest from him, never the same dear soul. Each line is aimed once and does not follow, so the chosen stepped aside. I stayed near, so it passed me by.",
      grovekeeper: "It ran through the farthest of us to the wall, like a gale along one furrow, and Corvane's followed through the farthest from him, never the same one. Each line is cast once and does not follow. The chosen stepped aside; my lot kept clear of both.",
      chronomancer: "A dash, then its echo: the Sovereign through whoever stands farthest, out to the wall, and next Corvane through whoever is farthest from him, a different soul each time. They do not follow once aimed. I was picked, and sidestepped as if bored. The rest kept off both.",
      tidecaller: "It ran through the farthest of us to the wall like a rip along the shore, and Corvane's followed, a breath later, through the farthest from him, never the same one. Each rip is drawn once and does not follow. The chosen stepped aside; the rest kept clear.",
      lanternkeeper: "It ran through the farthest of us to the wall, and Corvane's ran through the farthest from him a beat after, never the same one. Each line is drawn once and does not follow. I stayed in close, so no lamp of mine was in either, and the chosen stepped aside.",
      kenseier: "It ran through the farthest of us to the wall; a beat later Corvane ran through the farthest from him, never the same one. Each stroke is aimed once and does not follow. The chosen stepped off on its beat. A clean pair.",
      houndmaster: "Ran through whoever stood farthest, out to the wall, and Corvane a beat after, through whoever was farthest from him, never the same one. Lines aimed once, never follow. Had the dogs in close, and the one picked stepped off the line.",
    } },
    'Pyre': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "It numbered the places we stood when it began, then cut to each in turn, while Corvane came from the far end of the order, so two blades fell on every beat. All the lines were laid at once and none followed. I left my mark.",
      gloomsworn: "It took our places as they were, numbered them, and cut between them in order, Corvane from the other end. The lines were drawn at once and did not follow. I left my mark early. Standing still is how I died the first time.",
      thresher: "Our places were numbered the moment it began, dear, and the cuts ran between them, Corvane's from the far end, two blades a beat. Nothing follows once it is drawn. I left my mark at once.",
      bloodreaver: "It marked where each of us stood and cut between the marks in order, with Corvane running the order backwards, two blades a beat! The lines never follow. Stepping off my mark felt like a waste of a good wound. I'll wait for a better one.",
      runeguard: "Numbers lie on the places where we stood when it began. Its cuts run between them in order, and Corvane's from the far end, so two fall each beat. Nothing follows; leave the marked place and keep off every line.",
      gorgemaw: "It numbered the seats we sat in and carved between them in order, with Corvane carving from the far end, two blades a course. The cuts were laid on the table at once. I left my seat before the first.",
      oathbroker: "Our places at the start are numbered, and the cuts run between them in order, Corvane's from the far end, two blades a beat. None follows the signatory once drawn. Do not remain on your mark.",
      ironpalm: "It numbered the places where we stood, and cut between them in order. Corvane answered from the other end, two blades on each beat. Nothing followed. I left my place early and kept off the lines.",
      ranger: "Marks laid where we stood at the start, numbered. Cuts run between them, Corvane from the far end, two blades a beat. Nothing follows. Left my mark, kept off the lines, drew from clear ground.",
      shadowblade: "It numbered the places we stood when it began, and cut between them in order, Corvane working back from the far end. Nothing follows once drawn. I slipped off my mark first, as I do.",
      pyromancer: "It numbered where we stood, and cut between the numbers in order, and Corvane cut them back from the far end, two blades a beat! Nothing follows. I left my mark before the floor could object.",
      thermist: "Our places, numbered, and cuts between them in order, Corvane from the far end, two blades a beat. Nothing follows. I left my mark at once and kept off every line, hands shaking all the same.",
      driftblade: "It numbered where we'd stood and cut between the numbers in order, Corvane cutting back from the far end, and nothing followed us, so I left my mark early and went round the lines, light on my feet.",
      alembist: "Our places at the start, numbered, then cut between in order, Corvane cutting from the far end, two blades a beat. Leave the marked place, keep clear of every line. Nothing on the shelf is worth standing on.",
      threadweaver: "It basted a line between the places where we stood, in order, and Corvane basted back from the far end, two blades a beat. The stitches don't follow once they're laid. I left my mark and drew clear.",
      marionettist: "It numbered our marks, the places we stood at curtain up, and cut between them in order, Corvane working back from the last. Nothing follows once the lines are drawn. I left my mark before the cue. (The Doll was already in the wings.)",
      cleric: "It numbered the places where we stood, and cut between them in order, and Corvane cut them back from the far end, two blades a beat. Nothing follows. I called everyone off their marks first, and no one lingered.",
      grovekeeper: "The cuts ran from mark to mark like frost along furrows, in order, and Corvane's ran back from the far end, two blades a beat. Marks are where we stood, and nothing follows. I left mine and kept clear.",
      chronomancer: "Our places at the start were numbered, and the cuts run between them in order, with Corvane's from the far end, two on each count. Nothing follows. Leave your mark before the first, not on it. I had left it already, or I will have.",
      tidecaller: "It numbered the places where we stood, and the cuts ran between them like two currents crossing, Corvane's from the far end. Nothing follows once drawn. I left my place before the first cut.",
      lanternkeeper: "It numbered the places where we stood, and cut between them in order, Corvane working back from the far end. Nothing follows. I picked my lamp up and set it down well clear of every line.",
      kenseier: "Our places at the start were numbered, and the strokes ran between them in order, Corvane's from the far end, two to a beat. None follows. I left my mark before the count began.",
      houndmaster: "It numbered the places we stood, and cut between them in order, Corvane from the far end, two blades each beat. Nothing follows. Left my mark, whistled the dogs off theirs, and kept clear of the lot.",
    } },
    'Two Dancers': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Caelum stood at its side and laid down numbered afterimages, each a place it would stand, in order. On the last beats he would step in and cut beside it. Two blades, both shown. I learned the order from the numbers and kept my feet off the lines between.",
      gloomsworn: "Caelum stood apart and laid down his afterimages, numbered, each a place it would stand in turn, and said nothing. On the last beats he would step in and cut beside it. I read the numbers and kept off the lines. A ghost knows another's footprints.",
      thresher: "Caelum stood up so prettily, dear, and left his afterimages about the room, each numbered, each a place it would be, in order. On the last of them he would step in and cut beside it. Two blades, both shown before the first. Read the numbers and mind the step.",
      bloodreaver: "Caelum stood clear and laid out his afterimages, numbered, each one a place it would strike from, in order! On the last of them he'd step in and cut beside it, two blades, both shown before the first. I learned the order from the numbers. A good dance, and I wasn't in it.",
      runeguard: "Caelum stands apart and sets down numbered afterimages, each marking where he will be; learn the order. On the last beats he steps in and cuts beside it, two blades at each beat, both shown before the first. Keep off the lines.",
      gorgemaw: "Caelum stood up like a maître d' and set out his afterimages, numbered, each a place my host would stand, in order. For the last courses he would step in and cut beside it, two blades, both shown before the first. I read the menu and kept off the plates. Elegant.",
      oathbroker: "Caelum arose and laid down his afterimages, numbered, each a place it will stand in turn. On the last beats he would countersign, stepping in to cut beside it: two blades, both shown before the first. I read the schedule and kept off the lines.",
      ironpalm: "Caelum stood apart and left his afterimages, numbered, each a place it would stand, in turn. Near the end he would step in and cut beside it, two blades, both shown first. I read the numbers like a sentence. I did not need to rush.",
      ranger: "Caelum off to one side, laying down afterimages. Numbered, each a spot it'll stand, in sequence. At the end he steps in and cuts alongside it: two blades, both shown first. Learned the numbers, stayed off the lines.",
      shadowblade: "Caelum stood apart and left afterimages about the room, numbered, each one a place it will be, in order. Someone else making a show of being unseen. On the last beats he steps in and cuts beside it. I read the numbers and kept off the lines.",
      pyromancer: "Caelum stood up all silver and shining and left his afterimages, numbered, each a place it would stand, in order! On the last beats he'd step in and cut beside it, two blades, both shown first. I read the numbers and kept clear of the lines. A fine light show.",
      thermist: "Caelum stands apart, afterimages numbered, each a place he will stand, in order. Then, on the last beats, he steps in and cuts beside it, two blades, both shown before the first. I learned the order from the numbers and kept off the lines, trembling with it.",
      driftblade: "Caelum stood apart and laid his afterimages round the room, numbered, so I knew the route before it moved, and wanted to run it. But on the last beats he would step in and cut beside it, so I stayed off the lines instead.",
      alembist: "A dance nobody can see coming, until Caelum stands apart and lays down afterimages, numbered, each a place he will stand, in order. On the last beats he steps in and cuts beside it, two blades, both shown before the first. Read the labels, keep off the lines.",
      threadweaver: "Caelum stood apart and left his afterimages like tacking stitches, numbered, each a place it would stand, in order. On the last beats he would step in and cut beside it, two blades, both shown before the first. I read the pattern and kept off the seams.",
      marionettist: "Caelum took his mark upstage and laid his afterimages like a script, numbered, each a place our star would stand, in order. On the last beats he steps in and cuts beside him: two blades, both shown before the first cut. (I read the blocking twice. The Doll read it once.)",
      cleric: "Caelum stood apart and laid his afterimages round the hall, numbered, each a place it would stand, in order. On the last beats he would step in and cut beside it, two blades, both shown before the first. I learned the order from the numbers and kept everyone clear.",
      grovekeeper: "Caelum sowed his afterimages round the room like seed, each numbered, each a place it would stand, in order. Come the last beats he would cut beside it, and both blades were shown first. Read the rows.",
      chronomancer: "Caelum stood apart and sowed numbered echoes of himself, each a place he would stand next, in order. At the end he stepped into one and cut beside it, two blades, both shown ahead of the first. I counted the numbers like a score and stayed off the lines.",
      tidecaller: "Caelum stood apart and left his afterimages about the room like beacons, numbered, each a place he would stand, in order. When the last beats came he stepped in and cut beside it, two blades, both lit before the first. I read the numbers like a harbour chart and kept off the lines.",
      lanternkeeper: "Caelum left his afterimages like lamps along a road, each numbered, the road it would walk. On the last beats he would step in and cut beside it, both blades shown first. I read the road and kept off it.",
      kenseier: "Caelum stood apart and laid his afterimages round the room, numbered: the count of the dance, given away in advance. On the last beats he would step in and cut beside it, two strokes to a beat, both shown before the first. I learned the count and kept off the lines.",
      houndmaster: "Caelum stood off to one side and left his afterimages about the room, numbered, each a place the big lad would stand in turn. On the last beats he'd step in and cut beside him, two blades, both shown first. Read the numbers, dogs off the lines.",
    } },
    'Foretold': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Four ghosts of us rose with marks over their heads, and three blades were coming out of the middle. I walked into my own ghost and held it. The blue marks, mine and our healer's, were taken together; the seed opened a hole where its ghost stood.",
      gloomsworn: "The dead showed us ahead of time: four ghosts of us, each marked. A ghost knows where its own walks. I went into mine before the three blades left the middle, and only the gaps held. Two shared a blue mark. The seed's hole opened under its ghost.",
      thresher: "Such thoughtful ghosts, dear: four of us, each with a little mark, each place set before the blades. Into your own, then, and not a step off. The blue pair share their hit; the seed opens its hole right where its ghost stood.",
      bloodreaver: "Ghosts of the four of us, my friend, each marked, and three blades came out of the middle! I ran into my own and stood fast; nothing else was left standing. The blue pair split theirs, the red one took its own, and the seed dug a hole under its ghost.",
      runeguard: "Four ghosts of us, each with its mark. Stand in your own before the three blades leave the middle, since only the gaps they leave are clear. The blue pair share their blow, the red one bears its own, and the seed opens a hole under its ghost.",
      gorgemaw: "Four ghosts set out like places at table, each one marked, and my host sent three blades from the middle to clear away everything else. I sat in my own place and did not stir. The blue pair shared a plate; the seed's place was left a hole.",
      oathbroker: "The terms were shown in advance: four ghosts of us, each marked, and three blades from the middle voiding all ground but theirs. I took my own ghost's place before they fell. The blue pair share one obligation, the red one bears its own, the seed opens a hole where its ghost stood.",
      ironpalm: "The ghosts came first, four of us, each marked, and I did not rush. I walked to my own and stood still while three blades crossed the room from the middle. The blue pair took theirs together, the red alone, and the seed opened a hole beneath its ghost.",
      ranger: "Four ghosts, one for each of us, marked. Three blades from the middle cut everything but the gaps they leave. Walked into mine early, held still. Blue pair share, red takes its own, seed's hole opens under its ghost.",
      shadowblade: "Four ghosts of us, each marked, and nobody was meant to wander off from theirs, which I resented. I slipped into mine before the three blades left the middle and, for once, stayed put. The blue pair share, the red one bears its own, the seed's hole opens beneath its ghost.",
      pyromancer: "Ghosts of us! Four, each with a mark, standing in the gaps of three blades from the middle! I ran straight into mine and stood there, trembling with enthusiasm. The blue pair shared theirs, the red one took its own, and the seed burned a hole under its ghost.",
      thermist: "Four ghosts of us, each marked, and three blades from the middle that left only the gaps. I walked into mine early and stayed, as the ghost of me would. Blue pair share theirs, the red one takes its own, the seed's hole opens beneath it. Hold steady.",
      driftblade: "Four ghosts of us stood in the gaps before three blades came out of the middle, each marked, and I wanted to drift off but walked into my own and stayed, which is harder than it sounds. The blue pair shared their blow, and the seed opened a hole under its ghost.",
      alembist: "Three blades from the middle, and no safe ground but the gaps. Four ghosts of us, each marked, show where; walk into your own and stay. The blue pair share one dose, the red one takes its own, and the seed leaves a hole where its ghost stood.",
      threadweaver: "Four ghosts of us basted into the room, each tagged, and three blades cut every other seam from the middle. I walked into my own stitch and held it. The blue pair share a thread, the red one has its own, the seed leaves a hole.",
      marionettist: "Four ghosts took their marks before the cue: understudies of us, each tagged, standing in the gaps of three blades from the middle. I walked into mine and held my place. (The Doll had found its own first.) The blue pair share a scene, and the seed leaves a hole where its ghost stood.",
      cleric: "Four ghosts of us rose, each marked, and I walked into mine before the three blades came out of the middle, for nowhere else was left. Our shield and I shared the blue, and the red one bore its own. The seed opened a hole where its ghost stood.",
      grovekeeper: "Four ghosts of us rooted in the hall, each marked, and three blades came through from the middle like a plough. I walked into my own and put down roots. The blue pair share their burden, the red one has its own, and the seed leaves a hole where its ghost grew.",
      chronomancer: "I saw it a moment early: four ghosts of us, each marked, and three blades from the middle. I walked into mine before they came, and stood still. The blue pair share their blow, the red one bears its own, and the seed's hole opens beneath its ghost.",
      tidecaller: "Four ghosts of us rose like tidemarks, each with its own mark, and three blades came across from the middle like a wave. Only the gaps held. I walked into my own ghost and stayed. The blue pair share their blow, and the seed opened a hole where its ghost stood.",
      lanternkeeper: "Four ghosts of us stood where lamps would go, each marked, and three blades came from the middle to put out all else. I walked into my own ghost and kept still. The blue pair shared a light, the red one bore its own, the seed left a hole.",
      kenseier: "Four ghosts of us, each marked, are the count given in advance. Three blades come out of the middle on the beat and take all but the gaps. I walked into my own before they fell. Blue pair share the strike, red takes its own, seed's hole opens under its ghost.",
      houndmaster: "Four ghosts of us showed up first, each marked, and three blades came out of the middle after. Whistled the dogs clear, walked into my own and stood. Blue pair split theirs, red takes its own, seed opens a hole where its ghost stood. Rust sat on his ghost's feet.",
    } },
    "The Sovereign's Dance": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Six cuts, one after another, image to numbered image, and the brightest line was always the next to fall. At the end Caelum cut beside it, mirrored through the middle, so two lines came on each of the last three beats. I took them one beat at a time and checked both.",
      gloomsworn: "The blade went from image to image in the numbered order, six cuts, and I watched whichever line burned brightest. Caelum joined the last three, turned half about the room, so two fell together. A beat at a time. The dead are patient dancers.",
      thresher: "Six cuts, dear, and the brightest line was always the next guest due. Caelum joined in for the last three from the far side of the floor, so two lines came at once. Off the one, off its twin, and on to the next. A tidy step.",
      bloodreaver: "Six cuts, image to image, in order, and I went wherever the bright line wasn't! Then Caelum joined the last three from across the middle, two blades on a beat. Check both, my friend. Slipping a pair felt grand.",
      runeguard: "Six cuts in the numbered order, the brightest line the one next due; leave each in turn. Caelum joins the last three, his lines set opposite the others through the middle, so two fall together, and both must be read.",
      gorgemaw: "Six slices, one numbered image to the next, and the brightest line was the next dish out. For the last three, Caelum carved from the opposite side of the table, two blades each course. I kept clear of both. Quite a tasting.",
      oathbroker: "Six cuts in the numbered order; each line falls in its turn, the brightest next. On the last three beats Caelum cuts a second set, the same lines carried across the middle, so two fall together. Honour each beat as it comes.",
      ironpalm: "Six cuts, each a line, the brightest speaking next. I answered each in turn and moved after it. On the last three, Caelum spoke from the far side, two at once. I checked both.",
      ranger: "Six cuts through the numbered images. Bright line falls next. Last three, Caelum brings a second line from the far side, same beat. Move one beat at a time, check both.",
      shadowblade: "Six cuts between the numbered images, and each line announced itself, brightest first, which is terribly bad manners. Then Caelum cut alongside for the last three, from the opposite side. I watched both and slipped aside.",
      pyromancer: "Six cuts of light, image to image, brightest first, and I gave way to each one. Then for the last three beats Caelum cut from the far side, TWO at a time! Look for the twin before you leap.",
      thermist: "Six cuts, bright line next, step off. The last three come doubled, Caelum's lines mirrored through the middle, and I nearly ran into the twin, flushed with haste. Check both before leaping.",
      driftblade: "Six cuts and I was through each line as it lit and round to the next image, until Caelum joined for the last three from across the floor, two at once, and I had to slow down and look before I went.",
      alembist: "Six cuts, one numbered image to the next. Leave each bright line as it lands. The last three beats are doubled, Caelum cutting the same lines from the opposite side. Check the twin before dosing.",
      threadweaver: "Six seams, image to numbered image, the brightest the next to be sewn, and I stepped off each. On the last three, Caelum sewed a second seam from the far side, so two at once. Mind both.",
      marionettist: "Six cuts, image to numbered image, each the next cue as it brightened. The last three had a second lead: Caelum, entering from the opposite wing. (Two blades, one beat; I watched both.) The company took each beat in turn.",
      cleric: "Six cuts, one after another, the brightest the next to fall. On the last three Caelum cut beside it, from across the middle, so two came at once. We stepped off both in turn. I mended what clipped us.",
      grovekeeper: "Six cuts across the floor like furrows, the brightest the next to be ploughed. For the last three Caelum ploughed a second row from the far side. Two at once. I stepped clear of each in turn, and checked its twin.",
      chronomancer: "Six cuts in numbered order, the bright line falling next. The last three beats are doubled, with Caelum cutting from the far side. I stepped off one on its beat and then the other. I was clear before I left.",
      tidecaller: "Six cuts, each a rip across the floor, the brightest breaking next. I let each one pass. On the last three Caelum's rips came from the far shore, two on a beat. Watch both.",
      lanternkeeper: "Six cuts, one numbered image to the next, and the lit line fell next. I kept off it. The last three beats came in twos, Caelum cutting from the far side. Two lines, one beat.",
      kenseier: "Six strokes, one to a beat, the bright line next. I stepped off each on its beat. The last three were doubled, Caelum's strokes coming from the far side. Count both. Honest, if showy.",
      houndmaster: "Six cuts, image to image, bright line falls next. Last three come doubled, Caelum cutting from the far side. Dogs off the bright one, then off its twin.",
    } },
    'Sunwheel of Embers': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Brannoc's wheel came with its road painted out, three bounces and a blue ring. Each leg left a wall of fire. I stood in the ring and took the catch, a hard blow, and the fire died. Someone has to. The others kept off the road.",
      gloomsworn: "The wheel ran its painted road and left walls of fire behind each leg. I stood in the blue ring at the end and took it whole, and the fire went out. I have taken heavier blows from the living. Someone has to stand there, or we all pay.",
      thresher: "Brannoc's wheel bounced three times, dear, and every leg burned behind it. Someone must stand in the blue ring where it stops and catch it. It hurts, but the fire goes out at once. The rest of us kept off the road.",
      bloodreaver: "Brannoc hurled the wheel, my friend, and it bounced three times and left fire along every leg! I stood in the blue ring and caught it, and WHAT a hit, and the walls went out. No catch means a blast for everyone.",
      runeguard: "A road painted, three bounces, a blue ring at its end, fire left along each leg. One stands in the ring and takes the catch, heavily, whereupon the walls go out. Uncaught, it breaks on all of us.",
      gorgemaw: "A burning platter, tossed three times off the walls, and every leg left a wall of fire. Someone must receive it in the blue ring, a heavy dish, and then the fire goes out. The rest of us kept off the road. Filling.",
      oathbroker: "Brannoc's wheel: the road drawn in advance, three bounces, a blue ring at the end, a wall of fire along each leg. One party must stand in the ring and receive it, at heavy cost, and the walls are discharged. Else all pay.",
      ironpalm: "The wheel was thrown, and its road was painted before it moved. One of us stood in the blue ring at the end and caught it. It was heavy, and the fire went out. Without a catch, all of us pay.",
      ranger: "Wheel's road painted: three bounces, blue ring at the end, fire left on every leg. Someone catches it in the ring, takes a hard hit, and the walls go out. No catch, everyone pays. Stayed off the road.",
      shadowblade: "Brannoc showed us the wheel's whole road before he threw it, which is generous of him. Three bounces, a blue ring, fire along each leg. One of us caught it in the ring, and the walls went out. The rest of us kept off.",
      pyromancer: "Brannoc hurled a wheel of embers, and fire I did not set! Three bounces, a wall of flame left along each leg, and a blue ring where it stopped. One of us caught it there, and the walls went out. Keep off the road.",
      thermist: "The road painted, three bounces, a blue ring at the end. Fire along every leg, and one of us in the ring to catch it, hard, and the walls go out. No catch, all pay. Kept off the road.",
      driftblade: "The wheel's whole road was painted, and I kept off it and out of the burning legs behind it, which was just as well, because one of us, not me, thank the wind, had to stand still in the blue ring and catch it.",
      alembist: "A wheel of embers, three bounces, fire left on each leg. One of us stands in the blue ring and takes the catch, heavily, and the walls go out. No catch, and all of us pay. Keep off the road. Handle with gloves.",
      threadweaver: "The wheel ran its painted road like a spool unwinding, three bounces, fire left along each leg. One of us stood in the blue ring and caught it, hard, and the walls went out. I tied the catcher up first.",
      marionettist: "Brannoc hurled his wheel across the stage, its road painted in advance, three bounces and a blue ring. One of the company caught it there, hard, and the fire went out. (The Doll kept to the wings, off the road.)",
      cleric: "Brannoc threw a wheel of embers, and the whole road was painted, with a blue ring at the end. One of us caught it there, a heavy blow, and the walls of fire went out. I mended the one who caught it, afterwards. No catch, and all of us pay.",
      grovekeeper: "The wheel's road burned behind it like a hedgerow in a dry year, three legs of it. One of my lot stood in the blue ring and caught it, hard, and the fire died away at once. No catch, and all of us pay.",
      chronomancer: "The road is painted before the wheel moves, three bounces, a blue ring, fire along each leg. One of us catches it in the ring, hard, and the walls go out. I was off the road already.",
      tidecaller: "The wheel broke three times off the wall like a wave on rock, and each leg burned behind it. One of us caught it in the blue ring, hard, and the fire went out at once. No catch, and all of us pay.",
      lanternkeeper: "The wheel's road was painted, three legs, each one burning after it passed. I kept my lamps off it. One of us stood in the blue ring and caught the wheel, hard, and every wall of fire went out. No catch, and we all pay.",
      kenseier: "Three bounces, and the road painted before the throw. Each leg burned after the wheel had passed. One of us met it in the blue ring, hard, and the walls went out. The road kept good time.",
      houndmaster: "Brannoc chucked a burning wheel, road painted first, three bounces. Fire left on every leg. One of us stood in the blue ring and caught it, hard, and it all went out. No catch, we all pay. Dogs off the road.",
    } },
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------- the rest of the Fall: the grace leaving, the bell, and Morrow-Oak's seeds (a hero's own seed never lands on a shield)
    'Grace Withdrawn': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The grace left every one of us at once, and I stood on the last thread of my own life. Nothing struck. I stayed close to the healer and out of everything until we were mended, because the dark was coming.",
      gloomsworn: "It drew the life out of all of us, down to a thread, and nothing hit. I know that thread; I have stood on it for years. We kept clear of everything and stayed near the healer, and let the mending come before the dark did.",
      thresher: "The tall gentleman took the grace from every one of us, dear, and left us hanging by a thread, with no blow at all. The trick is to keep out of everything and let the healer fill us back up. Do mind where you step.",
      bloodreaver: "It took everything out of me and everyone, my friend, down to a thread, and not one blow came with it! Hard to love, that. Keep out of the fire, keep close to the healer, and be mended before the dark. Barely a scratch, and it nearly broke me.",
      runeguard: "The grace is drawn out of each of us, and we stand on a single thread with no blow upon it. Touch nothing, stay near the healer, and be mended in full before the dark that follows. I had seen it twice before I understood it once.",
      gorgemaw: "It cleared every plate at once and left us the last crumb, and no blow came with it. We kept out of everything and gathered by the healer to be refilled before the next course. A cruel little amuse-bouche.",
      oathbroker: "The grace is withdrawn from every party at once, leaving all on a single thread, with no blow attached. The clause: stand clear of everything, remain near the healer, and be mended in full before the second instalment falls.",
      ironpalm: "It took the strength out of every one of us and left a thread, and nothing struck. I stood still, away from everything, close to the healer, and breathed while we were mended. Then the bell came.",
      ranger: "Grace gone. Everyone down to a thread, no blow. Stayed off everything, closed up near the healer, waited to be topped up. Worst time to take one step wrong.",
      shadowblade: "It took the grace from all of us, which is no way to treat a guest, and left us on a thread. Not a blow landed. I stood by the healer, off everything, and waited to be mended. If I flinched, nobody saw.",
      pyromancer: "It put every one of us down to a thread, and I felt my flames gutter! No blow, though. I stood still beside the healer, out of every fire, and was mended before the dark. A near thing, and a quiet one.",
      thermist: "The grace is gone and everyone is down to a thread. No blow came. Stay out of everything, stay near the healer, be mended before what follows. I ran hot with fright, then went still.",
      driftblade: "It took the grace from all of us and left a thread and nothing hit, so I did not run, which I hate, and stayed by the healer and off everything while the mending came, and not one of us moved until it was done.",
      alembist: "Grace withdrawn, the whole party down to a thread, no blow. Keep out of everything, gather near the healer, be mended in full before the dark follows. Big flask, early. Nobody dies of this, if they stand still.",
      threadweaver: "It drew every thread in the party down to the last strand, and no blow came with it. I kept my stitches close and out of everything, and mended each of them in full before the dark came.",
      marionettist: "Hush, and the whole company on a single thread, no blow at all! Our star wanted us still. We stood off every mark, close to the healer, and were mended in the wings before the next scene. (Hold. Hold. The Doll held.)",
      cleric: "It drew the life out of every one of us and left a thread, and no blow came. I gathered everyone close and kept them out of everything, and mended them as fast as I could before the dark.",
      grovekeeper: "It felt like a drought drawing every root dry at once, everyone down to a thread, and no blow. They stayed close to me and off everything while I planted the cure, quick, before the dark.",
      chronomancer: "It takes everything back at once, and I will have been ready. Everyone on a thread, no blow, and what follows a few beats after. They stayed close and clear of everything, and I mended each one in time. Almost in time.",
      tidecaller: "It drew the water out of us all at once, the sea run to the mud, and nothing struck. They stood close and clear of everything while I raised us again, before the dark.",
      lanternkeeper: "Every light we had went down to a thread, and no blow came. I gathered the party close, kept them clear of everything, and lit every wick again before the dark. One apiece, and no dawdling.",
      kenseier: "A moment with no beat and nothing to turn: the grace drawn from every one of us, a thread left. We stood off everything and close to the healer, and were mended. Some moments are only outlasted.",
      houndmaster: "It took everybody's strength at a stroke, dogs too, down to a thread, and no blow. Kept clear of everything, called the dogs in tight by the healer, got mended.",
    } },
    'Nightfall': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Nightfall tolled, slower than a heart, and then it fell on every one of us at once. No shield was any use. We began on the thread, so the healer had to have us all full before the last toll. We stood shoulder to shoulder and took it.",
      gloomsworn: "The bell tolled and tolled, and then the dark came for every one of us together. No wall, no door, nothing to put between us and it. The mending had to be finished before the last toll, and it was. We took it close beside the healer. I have stood worse watches.",
      thresher: "A great bell, dear, and then Nightfall on all of us at once. Everyone must be mended to the brim before the last toll or the evening ends early. Keep close by your healer, dears, and no wandering. It is the whole point.",
      bloodreaver: "The bell tolled and tolled, my friend, and then the dark hit every one of us together! If you aren't mended by the last toll, you fall. The healer filled us all up, and we took it as one. The hardest blow I ever loved.",
      runeguard: "A bell tolled, each toll nearer the last, and Nightfall reached every one of us. Be mended in full before the final toll, gather close to the healer, let nothing else touch us meanwhile. Heavy, but I had written it down.",
      gorgemaw: "A bell, and then Nightfall: a dark course for every seat at once. Everyone must be filled to the brim before the last toll, close beside the healer. A cruel thing, to starve a party and then serve it a banquet.",
      oathbroker: "The bell tolls, and Nightfall falls due on all parties together. Each of us must be mended in full before the last toll, gathered near the healer, or default is certain. The hardest clause in the realm.",
      ironpalm: "The bell tolled, each toll slower than a heartbeat, and then the dark spoke to all of us at once. We stood close, mended in full before the last toll, and took it together. I kept listening until the dark lifted.",
      ranger: "Bell tolls, then Nightfall hits everyone. Need the healer to have us all full before the last toll. Stood close and still. No gap to slip. Barely.",
      shadowblade: "The bell tolled, and then Nightfall reached every one of us with no shadow to hide in, which was rude of it. The healer had to top us all up before the last toll. I stood close to her and waited, and it found me anyway.",
      pyromancer: "A bell, and then Nightfall, on every one of us! I stood very close to the healer and was mended to the brim before the last toll. I did not blink once. Flowers for the healer, later.",
      thermist: "A bell tolling, then Nightfall on everyone. Be mended in full before the last toll, close to the healer. I sweated through the waiting, and we passed it together.",
      driftblade: "The bell tolled and Nightfall was going to reach everyone and there was nowhere to run, so I ran in to the healer instead, through the others, and was mended and stood close and took it with them.",
      alembist: "Nightfall, on everyone at the last toll of the bell. Mend all of us to the brim beforehand, gathered close to the healer. Brew before, never after. The hardest dose on the shelf.",
      threadweaver: "The bell tolled, and Nightfall reached every thread at once. I drew my stitches in close and mended each one in full before the last toll, and let it run through us together. The hardest seam of my life.",
      marionettist: "The great bell, and then Nightfall fell on the whole house at once. The company gathered near the healer, mended to the brim before the last toll, and took it together. (Bows to the healer, who earned them.)",
      cleric: "The bell tolled and Nightfall reached every one of us together. I gathered them close so one prayer could cover them all, and mended each to the brim before the last toll. I was quick, and I am not ashamed to say so.",
      grovekeeper: "Nightfall came like a hard frost after a still evening, on all of us at once, and the bell tolled it in. I gathered my lot close and planted the cure on every one before the last toll. A late mend is no mend.",
      chronomancer: "The bell tolls and Nightfall follows, on everyone, and I will have mended all of them by the last toll. They stood close to me for it. The hardest beat of the fight, and I kept it.",
      tidecaller: "A bell, and then Nightfall broke over every one of us, the last wave of a storm. I raised the water on all of them at once, so it was high when the dark came. Close together.",
      lanternkeeper: "The bell tolled, and Nightfall reached every one of us together. I lit every wick again, all at once, before the last toll, and the party stood close to my lamp.",
      kenseier: "The bell tolled slow and Nightfall fell on all of us. No beat a blade can turn, so we drew in close and were mended in full before the last toll, and outlasted it.",
      houndmaster: "Bell tolled, then Nightfall hit the lot of us, dogs too. Healer had to fill us all up before the last toll, so we stood in tight beside her. Steady. All of us standing in it.",
    } },
    'Seeds of Ash': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Morrow-Oak's seeds went to the others in turn, never to me. Each carrier walked theirs out to the rim, never the middle, and where it burst the floor burned through and stayed that way. I cut down what climbed out before it reached the tall one, or a name was called again.",
      gloomsworn: "The seeds passed to the living in turn and never to me. Each carried theirs to the rim, never the middle, and the floor burned through where it burst and stayed open. A burning sapling climbed out and walked for the tall one. We cut it down, or a name came again.",
      thresher: "A seed for each of you in turn, dear, and none for our shield. Carry yours to the rim, never the middle, for the floor burns through where it bursts and the hole stays. A little burning sapling climbs out. Cut it down before it reaches him, or a name comes again.",
      bloodreaver: "Seeds on the rest of us in turn, my friend, never the shield! I carried mine to the rim, never the middle, and the floor burned through and stayed burnt. A burning sapling climbed out and I chopped it down before it reached him, or a name comes back.",
      runeguard: "A seed for each of the others in turn, never the warder. Carry it to the rim, never the middle; the hole it burns stays for the whole fight. A sapling climbs out of it, and must be felled before it reaches him, or a name is called again.",
      gorgemaw: "A seed for each guest in turn, never the host. Carry yours out to the rim, never the middle, since the floor burns through where it bursts and the hole stays. A sapling climbs out, smoking. Eat it down before it reaches him, or a name is served again.",
      oathbroker: "The seeds fall to the other parties in turn, never to the shield. Clause: carry yours to the rim, never the middle; the floor burns through and the hole remains. A sapling emerges and must be felled before it reaches him, or a name is called again.",
      ironpalm: "The seeds went to the others in turn, never to me. Each walked theirs to the rim, never the middle, and the floor burned through and stayed open. I broke the sapling that climbed out before it reached him, or a name was called again.",
      ranger: "Seeds went on the rest of us, in turn. Not the shield. Carried mine to the rim, nowhere near the middle. Floor burns through where it bursts; hole stays. Sapling climbs out and walks at him: shot it down before it arrived, or a name comes again.",
      shadowblade: "A seed for each of us in turn, never the shield, which seemed unfair. I took mine to the edge of the room, not the centre, and the floor burned through behind me and stayed open. I cut down the sapling that climbed out before it reached him, or a name is called again.",
      pyromancer: "A seed on each of us in turn, never the shield, and what a glow! Carry it to the rim, never the middle: the floor burns through where it bursts and the hole stays. Put the sapling out before it reaches him, or a name is called again.",
      thermist: "Seeds, one by one, the shield spared. Take yours to the rim, never the middle, where the floor burns through and the hole stays. Fell the sapling that climbs out before it reaches him, or a name is called again. Rim first, then in.",
      driftblade: "A seed for each of us in turn, never the shield, and I ran mine to the rim, never the middle, which suited me, and the floor burned through behind me and the hole stayed, so I ran round it, and ran down the sapling before it reached him, or a name comes again.",
      alembist: "A seed, passed round the others in turn, never the shield. Bear it to the rim, not the middle; the floor burns through and the hole stays. A sapling climbs out of the hole: fell it before it reaches him, or a name is called again. Do not step in.",
      threadweaver: "Each of us in turn took a seed, the shield never. We drew ours out to the rim, never the middle, and where it burst the floor burned through and the hole stayed. The sapling that climbed out was cut before it reached him, or a name would sound again.",
      marionettist: "Our star scattered seeds, one to each of the company in turn, never the shield! We carried ours to the rim, never centre stage, and the floor burned through and the hole stayed. We cut the sapling down before it reached him, or a name came again. (The Doll kept clear.)",
      cleric: "Each of the others had a seed in turn, never our shield. They took them to the rim, never the middle, and I mended them after the burst; the floor burned through and the hole stayed. We felled the sapling before it reached him, else a name is called again.",
      grovekeeper: "Seeds, and I know seeds: one for each of the others in turn, never our shield. They carried them to the rim, never the middle, and the floor burned through and stayed bare. We cut the sapling down before it reached him, or a name is called again.",
      chronomancer: "A seed for each of the others in turn, never the shield, and I will have mended each carrier after. They carried them to the rim, never the middle; the hole stays. Cut the sapling down before it reaches him, or a name is called again. A tidy beat.",
      tidecaller: "Seeds on each of the others in turn, never the shield, dropped like pebbles in surf. They carried them to the rim, never the middle; the floor burned through and the hole stayed. We felled the sapling before it arrived, or a name comes again.",
      lanternkeeper: "A seed for each of the rest of us, the shield passed over. We walked ours out to the rim, and I lit them home. The floor burns through where it bursts and stays open. Cut down the sapling that climbs out before it gets to him, or a name sounds again.",
      kenseier: "The seeds came round in turn, never to the shield. Each carried theirs to the rim, never the middle, and the floor burned through and stayed open. The sapling walked in slow time. We cut it down before it reached him, or a name came again.",
      houndmaster: "Seeds go round the rest of us, not the shield. Took mine to the rim, never the middle, dogs heeled well back. Floor burns through where it bursts, hole stays. Sapling climbs out; cut it down before it reaches him, or a name comes again.",
    } },
    'The First Signal': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The first of the fallen to stand flares at his sigil, and that sets the room. The others answer on the same lines, turned the same way each time. Watch the first, and I know where to put my shield for the rest.",
      gloomsworn: "One sigil flared before the rest, and everything after it was laid on the same frame, only turned. The dead keep their pattern, as they always do. I read the first and walked to the next before it was called.",
      thresher: "The first sigil lights up, dear, and the rest of the guests answer in the same arrangement, just turned about the room. Same dance every time, and that's a kindness! Watch the first, then step ahead.",
      bloodreaver: "The first of them flares and the rest line up on the same plan, only swung round! Same swing every pull, my friend. I watch the first one land and I'm already walking to where the others will be. A good scar for the careless.",
      runeguard: "A single sigil flares first. Everything after it lies on the same frame, turned by the same measure every time. Read the first and the rest is already written in the margin.",
      gorgemaw: "The first dish out sets the whole service, and every course after it is laid the same way, only turned round the room. Same menu every pull. I taste the first and know where the rest are served.",
      oathbroker: "The first sigil to flare sets the terms, and every answer after it is entered on the same page, turned by the same amount, every time. Read the first clause and the others are already settled.",
      ironpalm: "One sigil flares, and the others follow it, turned the same way, always. I do not guess. I watch the first and let my feet find where the rest will fall.",
      ranger: "First sigil flares. The rest land on the same frame, turned. Same turn every pull. Read the first, walk to the next.",
      shadowblade: "It flares one sigil first, terribly proud of itself, and everything after is the same picture turned round, the same way every time. Predictable, which I forgive in no one. Read the first and be elsewhere.",
      pyromancer: "One sigil flares FIRST, and the rest of the fire answers on the same frame, swung round the room! Same swing every time, so I read the first and light my way to the next. Nobody outshines the first flare. Nobody.",
      thermist: "The first sigil flares and the rest sit on the same frame. Turned round, the same way every time, and I ran at the wrong one twice, scalded by my own haste. Read the first.",
      driftblade: "One sigil flared and I was already going, because everything after it was the same frame turned round the room, the same way every time, so you read the first and slide to where the next one will be and keep on moving.",
      alembist: "Three answers, and no order to read. The first to flare sets the frame, and the other two lie on it, turned the same way every time. Read the first and the rest is predictable. It is all in the turn.",
      threadweaver: "The first sigil flares, and the rest are stitched on the same pattern, turned round the room, the same turn every time. Read the first stitch and you know where the needle goes next.",
      marionettist: "The first sigil flares: our cue. Every answer after it is the same scene turned about the stage, the same turn every performance. (The Doll prefers to read the cue and be waiting in the wings.)",
      cleric: "The first of the fallen flares, and the rest follow on the same frame, turned the same way each time. I read the first, and told the others where to stand for the next. It helps to know.",
      grovekeeper: "The first sigil flares like a bud, and every answer after it grows on the same frame, turned round the room, the same turn every season. Read the first and you know where the rest will come up.",
      chronomancer: "The first sigil flares, and the rest follow on the same frame, turned by the same measure each time. A pattern is a little time you can borrow. Read the first, and stand in the next before it arrives.",
      tidecaller: "One sigil flares, and every answer after it breaks on the same shore, turned round the room by the same tide each time. Read the first wave and you know where the rest will land. Mind the ring.",
      lanternkeeper: "The first sigil flares, and the rest are laid on the same frame, turned round the room the same way every time. I watch the first light and walk toward where the next will fall.",
      kenseier: "The first sigil flares on the beat, and the rest fall on the same frame, turned the same way every pull. Honest. Read the first and you hold the count for the others.",
      houndmaster: "First sigil flares, lads, and the rest land on the same frame, swung round the same way every time. Read the first and the dogs are already at the next.",
    } },
    'Emberfall': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "It ran out of patience. Every seam of the floor went gold, the fire came up from beneath, and there was no ground left to hold. No shield for that. The only answer is to have it down before that begins.",
      gloomsworn: "The fire came up from under the stones, every seam gone gold, and ash closed in from the edges. The ground itself had turned on us. The only answer is to end it before it ends us. The watch has seen worse. Not often.",
      thresher: "The seams of the floor went gold, dear, and the fire came up from under us, and the ash closed in from the edges. Nobody can stand that. Do finish it before it starts. Oh, we did not.",
      bloodreaver: "My friend ran out of patience! The seams went gold, the fire came up from below, ash closed in, and not a scar of it was mine to keep. No hitting back at that. Kill him before it starts. Shameful, to fall to it.",
      runeguard: "Every seam in the floor goes gold, then the fire from beneath, then ash from the edges inward. There is no remedy but to have it ended before it begins. I did not see it in time; I have written it down now.",
      gorgemaw: "The whole hall went up from beneath: the seams gold, the fire under the floor, ash closing in from every edge. A dish with no seconds. Finish the cook before it is served.",
      oathbroker: "A final notice: the seams gold, the fire from beneath, the ash closing in from every edge. No clause protects any of us, and there is no appeal. The only remedy is the other party fallen before it falls due.",
      ironpalm: "The seams turned gold and the fire came up from under, and ash closed in from the edges. There was nothing to meet. I learned the only answer is to finish it before it begins. I listened to it come.",
      ranger: "Seams gone gold, fire from under the floor, ash closing in from the edges. No lane out. Only answer: down before it starts. Missed that window. Next time, sooner.",
      shadowblade: "The seams went gold and the fire came up from under, with no shadow left anywhere to stand in, and ash closed in from the edges. The only answer is to have it down before it starts. It saw all of us.",
      pyromancer: "The seams went gold and the fire came up from UNDER us, and I, who adore fire, had no opinion of it at all! Ash from the edges, nowhere to go. Finish it before it starts. Fully upstaged.",
      thermist: "The seams going gold. Then the fire coming up from under, ash closing in from the edges, no cold place left. Only answer: finish it before it begins. I ran out of cold and had nowhere to retreat to.",
      driftblade: "The seams went gold, and the fire came up from under, and I ran for the edge, and the ash was closing in from the edge, and there was no other way out. The only answer is to have it down before it starts.",
      alembist: "The seams gold, then fire from beneath, then ash from every edge. Nothing mends this once it begins, so it must be ended before it loses its patience. No cure after the dose.",
      threadweaver: "Every seam in the floor went gold, as if the whole hall were stitched in fire, and then it came up from beneath and the ash closed in from the edges. No thread holds that. End it before it begins.",
      marionettist: "(The seams of the floor lit gold, the fire rose from under the stage, and the ash drew in from the wings.) No part is written for us. The only answer is to end the play before this scene. The Doll took no bow.",
      cleric: "The seams went gold, and the fire rose from beneath us, and the ash closed in from the edges, and I had nothing to mend with. It must be ended before it starts, friends. I am grateful to those who tried.",
      grovekeeper: "The seams went gold and the fire came up from the roots, as a fire eats a hedge from under. Ash closed in from the edges. Nothing grows back from that. The only answer is to end it before it begins.",
      chronomancer: "I would have stopped it before the seams went gold. I let them. The fire came up from beneath and the ash closed in from the edges, and no beat of mine could hold it. End it before it begins. I should have.",
      tidecaller: "The seams went gold and the fire rose from beneath us, a flood coming in backwards, and the ash closed in from the edges. No harbour. The only answer is to finish it before the turn. We did not.",
      lanternkeeper: "Every seam in the floor lit gold, and the fire came up from under, and the ash closed in from the edges until my lamp was the smallest light there. Finish it before it begins.",
      kenseier: "The seams went gold, the fire came up from beneath, and the ash closed in from the edges. No beat a blade can turn. The only answer is to finish it before it begins. A fallen foe cannot burn the floor.",
      houndmaster: "Seams went gold, fire came up from under, ash closed in from the edges, dogs whining. No way out of it. Only answer is to have it down before it starts. Sorry, lads.",
    } },
  },
};
